{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies","id":876960,"title":"On Non-Iremite Mummies","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-28T14:14:43-07:00","id":876960,"title":"On Non-Iremite Mummies","content":"So, this thread is to discuss Non-Iremite Mummies; arguments for and against, how-to's on re-skinning the Mummy template for non-Egypt-ish cultures, how these could be represented by the 'amnesiac' mummies mentioned in the books, and any other topics our noodley little brains can come up with.<br /> <br /> So, to get the ball rolling, let's start with the aforementioned arguments on why or why not Mummies from beyond Irem should be a thing. My two cents: what if you're planning a game and, for whatever reason, you want to include a Mummy as a major player, or even just as a story event, and you don't want to muck about with explaining away Egypt-ish iconography and decor in, let's say, the middle of the Guatemalan rain forest? Sure, simply changing the description of the scene may be sufficient, replace the Egypt-ish themes with Mesoamerican-ish ones, but it's been my experience that keeping all of the little details straight and part of a cohesive whole can make all the difference.<br /> <br /> That's my argument 'for' (or one of them, anyway). Anyone have an 'against'?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T14:37:45-07:00","id":876986,"title":"","content":"Cults can change iconography. With the Arisen's poor memories, their flexible Sahu, and, if needed, the Twice-Arisen, you can already have all that.<br /> <br /> Mummies (as in the Arisen, Deceived, and Shuankhsen) came from the Rite of Return. What makes these other beings &quot;mummies&quot; such that they share the name with the previous ones?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T14:42:34-07:00","id":876992,"title":"","content":"I honestly have no problems with non-Iremite Mummies, but this game here is the wrong place for them. You should just build them as a Horror of some variety. The reason most players are against all Mummies falling under the one Mummy template is because the word &quot;Mummy&quot; in English is just used to refer to a preserved corpse. It can happen naturally, or as a consequence of a variety of different burial practices across the world. Each one of those practices is different enough that pretending they're all the same is enormously dismissive of the cultures in question, we only consider them even remotely equivalent because of that one linguistic quirk of English.<br /> <br /> So by all means, let's have rules for the animate corpses from different cultures. But not in this game. This game is about Egyptian Mummies specifically. If you want something else, that's fine, but not here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1059,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","user":"Azahul","date":".  Proper placement of apostrophes is important. 04-28-2016, 07:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T14:53:33-07:00","id":876999,"title":"","content":"I don't object to there being non-Iremite mummies. Just like I don't object to there being non-GM demons \u2014 in fact, last I checked, there were no less than five distinct things in the ChroD that are called \u201cdemons\u201d. <br /> <br /> In a similar way, non-Iremite mummies ought to be distinct from the Arisen, with enough differences that it doesn't make sense to use the same template even as a starting point. Sekhem, Memory, Pillars, Decrees, Affinities, and Utterances should all be specific to Irem's slaves; other mummies should be as different from them as the Purified from <b>Immortals</b> are.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T16:21:35-07:00","id":877066,"title":"","content":"These are all excellent points, but why reinvent the wheel? It's like the guidelines for converting mages to psychics or mad scientists in the <b>Mage Chronicler's Guide</b>; change some names, one or two rules tweaks, and Presto! You have the ever-dying functionaries of the Celestial Mandarins of P'en Lai!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T16:46:07-07:00","id":877093,"title":"","content":"For a game table hack, that works great. But there's a reason that's in the Chronicler's Guide - it's a setting hack, not something thematically consistent with the gameline as presented. I'd much rather see Taoist Mummies with their own system that emphasizes their themes rather than things like the Pillars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T17:17:58-07:00","id":877135,"title":"","content":"It'd still be about reclaiming memories, about maintaining one's self, leap-frogging between eras punctuated by periods of senescence...the dynamics would be essentially the same, just based off a different proto-culture. How would the themes be any different?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T17:19:23-07:00","id":877137,"title":"","content":"Irem had godlike beings with a fundamental understanding of the nature of Sekhem and a direct line to the gods, and they still had to sacrifice an entire civilization to make the Rite work. I really don't see later mortals pulling it off a second time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T18:05:35-07:00","id":877181,"title":"","content":"Who says it was a second time? Expansive as Irem was, and as unlikely as the timing would have been, it's possible that multiple proto-civs could have performed Rites of Return simultaneously, with each civ having its own patrons. Probable? Not in the least. But possible, especially in the ChroD, where unlikely (and horrifying) events occur all the time? Definitely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T18:20:10-07:00","id":877195,"title":"","content":"Can I just clarify what we're actually arguing here? Is it your right to make a fan-made setting hack for the game that just renames Mummy's stuff, or is it whether there should be canon non-Iremite Mummies in future expansions using the same basic mechanics?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T20:22:25-07:00","id":877275,"title":"","content":"Right now I think we're arguing the overall desirability of either.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T21:34:18-07:00","id":877316,"title":"","content":"I suppose a question: When we say non-Iremite mummies, do we mean non-Curse Mummies? Because if we want to run the hell away from what Curse's mummies are for those ideas, sure. But Curse's mummies are really tightly knit to the story of the Nameless Empire and it's mythology, and I think that's a really significant part of the strength of it, that this history, this story, goes down even into the mechanics.<br /> <br /> Also, I dislike how some people try to push non-Iremite to avoid playing ethnicities/cultures they have no reason to have a problem with, but that's a very minor group.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T22:15:16-07:00","id":877347,"title":"","content":"Also, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/32279-how-to-get-around-the-racial-restrictions?p=39949#post39949\">this</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T22:25:48-07:00","id":877358,"title":"","content":"Damnit, had a massive post but it got eaten. Let's see about the cliff-notes:<br /> <br /> -There's no problem if you just want to rename some stuff and claim your Mummy is from a different culture. Chronicles of Darkness are a toolkit, you can do anything you want with it.<br /> <br /> -That said, I really need to question why you think <i>Mummy: The Curse</i> is the best template for a non-Egyptian Mummified creature. Because I genuinely think the only reason to think that is because you're conflating such very different things together is because in English they're both called &quot;Mummies&quot;.<br /> <br /> -Horrors in 2ED, or maybe Abmortals or something in 1ED, would be a better baseline. Far fewer culturally specific rules and template concepts that need to be reworked from the ground up, far less flavour that needs to be thoroughly excised to make it work.<br /> <br /> On the subject of canon non-Iremite Arisen (as in Sekhem, low Memory, rise from death to serve eldritch masters for a short time based on the call of a relic and then back to sleep), that's even worse. All of the above applies, plus you've got the fact that it just doesn't fit into the lore. Look at all the very specific acts, from the murder of a god to the performance of a very particular ritual <i>rooted in Egyptian forms of mummification</i> that took place to create the Arisen. So many of the events of Irem were tied into the personalities of the Shan'iatu. The idea that the same thing could be replicated in China at about the same time is just preposterous. Particularly since the timing of the ritual itself is <i>also</i> rooted in the Egyptian concept of the Sothic cycle.<br /> <br /> Seriously though, I think if you want to proceed with creating Mummies from different cultures, you need to sit down and examine why you think the Arisen template makes a better jumping off point than, say, Vampires, Purified, Abmortals, or maybe even Promethean or Geist. Of all the creatures in the Chronicles of Darkness line, they have to be the one keyed most tightly to a specific culture, and consequently I think that makes them the <i>furthest</i> of all the types of undead or immortal beings in the line from &quot;Mummies&quot; from other cultures.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T22:39:39-07:00","id":877367,"title":"","content":"All that said, botched attempts at the Rite from other cultures (or just lone crazed weirdos, though those are probably more likely to create a Promethean than anything else) resulting in horrifying Lifeless that provoke a severe uncanny valley effect in the Arisen is a fantastic source of plot hooks. Something eerily like you, but running off of stolen Sekhem or missing several of the souls Pillars and just gushing Sybaris into the osychosohere around them? Yes please.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T22:43:46-07:00","id":877371,"title":"","content":"There's always also the Twice-Risen. I have an Arisen character who was completely bodily destroyed during the conquest of the first Caliphate, but was eventually resurrected through human sacrifice by the Inca (now inhabiting said sacrifice).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T23:19:59-07:00","id":877387,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=877367#post877367\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All that said, botched attempts at the Rite from other cultures (or just lone crazed weirdos, though those are probably more likely to create a Promethean than anything else) resulting in horrifying Lifeless that provoke a severe uncanny valley effect in the Arisen is a fantastic source of plot hooks. Something eerily like you, but running off of stolen Sekhem or missing several of the souls Pillars and just gushing Sybaris into the osychosohere around them? Yes please.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't know what other people want from &quot;non-Iremite Mummies&quot; but this is all I want. I'm more than okay with the major protagonists and antagonists being from Egypt, but some fringe canon fodder brute mutant mockeries are a nice way to contrast the &quot;refinement&quot; of the Rite of Return.<br /> <br /> Maybe the Shuankhsen fit that bill, which is why I constantly say it's hard for me to justify the topic. Still, if non-Iremite Mummies were created and not only ended up as Lifeless but as tools of the Devourer it would be a nice way to reinforce how She has her fingers in everything.<br /> <br /> But seeing other divine or cosmic beings influencing the world is cool, too. Something alien that even Deceived temakhs can't recall, but something glorious and radiant in its own way like the Iremite divinities","signature":"","edited":{"userid":638,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","user":"reseru","date":". 04-29-2016, 02:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T23:22:41-07:00","id":877388,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure more than a couple of Prometheans in history have been created in desperate attempts to pull off the Rite of Return. The Nepri especially, but probably some individual cases from other cultural backgrounds too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T23:29:20-07:00","id":877390,"title":"","content":"I am also a fan of non-Iremite mummies being decoupled from the Arisen template and general milleu. Things you can use for inspiration:<br /> <br /> - Horrors created whole cloth by the ST.<br /> - Prometheans, especially Osirans but the splat in general also could work.<br /> - 1e's Immortals, especially the Purified.<br /> - Revenants, the offshoot of ghosts.<br /> - 1e also made mention of Abmortals in Geist, although that wasn't particularly fleshed out.<br /> <br /> If I had to choose one method right now...well, honestly I'm already working on a Mummy/Geist/Promethean/Vampire setting and I think I'd probably make a Promethean Lineage to fit the bill.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage the Awakening 2e Communal Homebrew Threads:<br /> - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/966394-perils-of-the-path-post-your-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">Perils of the Path</a> - Other homebrew threads linked in the OP!<br /> <br /> Join us at the unofficial IRC channels on.irc.sorcery.net at #exalted and now #theonyxpath!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/451-kirby-jerusalem","name":"Kirby Jerusalem","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar451_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"589"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-28T23:36:41-07:00","id":877393,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=877181#post877181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who says it was a second time? Expansive as Irem was, and as unlikely as the timing would have been, it's possible that multiple proto-civs could have performed Rites of Return simultaneously, with each civ having its own patrons. Probable? Not in the least. But possible, especially in the ChroD, where unlikely (and horrifying) events occur all the time? Definitely.</div> </div> </div> </div>But why do you want that? What is gained by invoking such an improbable scenario that just beggars belief?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T00:06:13-07:00","id":877410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=877181#post877181\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Who says it was a second time? Expansive as Irem was, and as unlikely as the timing would have been, it's possible that multiple proto-civs could have performed Rites of Return simultaneously, with each civ having its own patrons. Probable? Not in the least. But possible, especially in the ChroD, where unlikely (and horrifying) events occur all the time? Definitely.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> What is the benefit of this, other than to make Irem and its mythos less significant?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T00:54:41-07:00","id":877441,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=877410#post877410\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What is the benefit of this, other than to make Irem and its mythos less significant?</div> </div> </div> </div>In fact: if the purpose is to make Irem and its mythos less significant, how is that a benefit?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-29-2016, 05:53 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T06:21:46-07:00","id":877623,"title":"","content":"The Iremite Mummies can be versatile without setting hacks if you use some creativity, as shown <b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/100839-exotic-mummies\">here</a></b>.<br /> <br /> That being said, taking issue with the Egyptian visuals and themes is like taking issue with the whole Frankenstein Lineage in Promethean, or Anglo-Saxon examples of fae in Changeling. Or to put it simply, it's like asking for a cake with no eggs or sugar. That's not a cake, no matter how you slice it (pun intended).<br /> <br /> I agree with using 2ED's Horror Creation System. Abmortals and Immortals are a good place to start as well. Sin-Eaters and Prometheans are not ageless by default, and making them so would have noticeable repercussions, such as more Krewes and less urgency to leave behind Athanors in 2ED.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Bye.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4443-gibberingeloquence","name":"GibberingEloquence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4443_5.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T06:26:58-07:00","id":877626,"title":"","content":"Something really important to me is the in-universe interpretations mortals have of the supernatural world. Regarding Vampires, for example, a forum poster once eloquently said how a mortal Vampire hunter in Tokyo would come to much different conclusions about Vampirism than one in Montreal, let alone encountering Strix or even blood spirits from the Shadow. How do you keep it all straight?<br /> <br /> So if we, in real life, are actually in-universe of <b>Mummy</b> how can we distinguish between, you know<ul><li>Arisen, Deceived, and Shuankhsen, who then might be so deluded they think they're native to Mesoamerica or just twice-arisen</li> <li>Sadikh and Fasad</li> <li>Random undead skeletons, zombies, ghosts, and walking corpses risen by relics, Affinities, or Utterances</li> <li>Sin-eaters, Prometheans, Vampires, Mage liches, Synthesized Strix, Purified, and whatever the fuck else?</li> </ul>So I'm with ya'll in a way. As far as we should really care that's a lot of 'mummified corpses.'<br /> <hr /><br /> But screw that.<br /> <br /> The Deathless are torn between the familiar - seeing the Nameless Empire's inheritances throughout history ('The US like bald eagles, but we invented animal symbols for our nation') - and the unfamiliar ('What is computer'). But Iremite Mummies, naturally, have a common connection. They all trace their origin to the Rite of Return. When the Arisen see a Shuankhsen they're like, &quot;Oh yeah, those guys. We've been fighting them for 5,000 years.&quot; They, in all likelihood, all have met and all know each other, barring Memory.<br /> <br /> They also all <i>serve</i>. They have a Purpose. The Judges, the temakhs, and the Devourer are all their masters. They have a Purpose.<br /> <br /> Now let's picture these non-Iremite Mummies. Why are they <i>Mummies</i> in <b>Mummy the Curse</b>?<br /> <br /> Possibly because they're <i>alien</i>. The ritualists who created them were inspired by the Rite of Return and the Deathless, and they called out into the depths of other worlds seeking patronage for their hubris. They found something radiant, beautiful, and divine, in the same way the Deathless recall Azar and the other gods of ancient Irem, except wholly <i>other</i>. Not Lovecraftian abomination other. The Iremite Mummies all have a common bond. But they share no common bond here. They are <i>other</i>. There is no familiarity. No sense of, &quot;Oh, what's up Shuankhsen? We meet again.&quot;<br /> <br /> But what if these Mummies had no Purpose? What if they could no longer serve because their patrons were Devoured? The Deathless anticipate Sothic turns as eras of freedom, vacations from service, and a glimpse of impossible freedom. What if these Mummies longed for it? What if Purpose reminded you - helped you reclaim Memory - what it is to be human? The Deathless want to follow their personal ambitions in their search for Apotheosis, but these other Mummies long for <i>anything</i> to fill the void that was stripped from them. They are torn between the embers of their patrons' former majesty and the puppeteering of their hollowed husks by darker forces.<br /> <br /> They're pitiable. They are what the Arisen fear they'll one day become, what they <i>know</i> they'll become: their masters obliterated, Duat lay in ruin, and so far distant from humanity that they'd be thankful just to be able to call themselves Deathless once again, for these non-Iremite Mummies are nothing but Lifeless","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T16:52:52-07:00","id":878234,"title":"","content":"Honestly, what you described is basically a Shuankhsen from the perspective of an Arisen. Like, pretty much exactly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-29T17:24:23-07:00","id":878256,"title":"","content":"Yeah, that's my difficulty with this. My imagination is having a hard time cracking this open because the game as is takes care of so many 'mummy' archetypes","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T05:45:08-07:00","id":878681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=877393#post877393\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But why do you want that? What is gained by invoking such an improbable scenario that just beggars belief?</div> </div> </div> </div>Truth be told, I don't necessarily want the civilizations happening side-by-side, so to speak, but I had an idea of a God-Machine plot opening rifts to timelines where Irem never existed, and the Rite was performed by other proto-civilizations. To that end, I'm trying to put together non-Iremite mummies with minimal mechanical divergence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T09:21:33-07:00","id":878795,"title":"","content":"I have never really had a problem with the Arisen having all been created with the same rite in ancient Irem. That said, it also hasn't stopped me from getting character ideas for mummies that seem to have come from another culture. For example, I had an idea for an ancient Roman mummy with a warrior cult that eventually evolved into an underground fight club in the modern day. My solution for his Roman features and love of the culture was to create this Merit:<br /> <br /> <b>Resonant Descent (...)</b><br /> <i>Prerequisite: </i>Memory 5<br /> The memories of most mummies are extremely limited, allowing them to gain only the smallest glimpses of their most recent Descents. There is something different about you, though. One of your lifetimes echoes through the ages, coming back to you time and time again. Something about this Descent has made it stick in your memory, whether it was the foundation of your cult, the beginning of a great love or rivalry, or even just a time of great personal satisfaction. Regardless of the reason, you can remember this lifetime as clearly as you can remember your most recent Descent. For some Arisen, this may even cause alterations to the sahu, causing the mummy to resemble a member of the most prevalent culture encountered during this lifetime. A mummy may only have one Resonant Descent, and this lifetime is never the mummy's original life in Irem.<br /> <br /> By doing that, I made it so that the character had originated in Irem like the others, but had become so attached to one of his (many) Roman lifetimes, that he'd basically gone native. I also thought that such a Merit would make sense to exist in the setting. Given the Swiss cheese Memory that the Arisen are said to possess, it had always struck me as odd that the mechanics seemed to make it so that the mummies would remember the most recent events and forget the older ones, rather than having an odd mix of ancient and modern memories.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T10:07:34-07:00","id":878835,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>White Oak Dragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=878795#post878795\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Resonant Descent (...)</b><br /> <i>Prerequisite: </i>Memory 5<br /> The memories of most mummies are extremely limited, allowing them to gain only the smallest glimpses of their most recent Descents. There is something different about you, though. One of your lifetimes echoes through the ages, coming back to you time and time again. Something about this Descent has made it stick in your memory, whether it was the foundation of your cult, the beginning of a great love or rivalry, or even just a time of great personal satisfaction. Regardless of the reason, you can remember this lifetime as clearly as you can remember your most recent Descent. For some Arisen, this may even cause alterations to the sahu, causing the mummy to resemble a member of the most prevalent culture encountered during this lifetime. A mummy may only have one Resonant Descent, and this lifetime is never the mummy's original life in Irem.</div> </div> </div> </div>This looks very promising to me. Has it come up that an Iremite mummy has tried to correct your Roman's...misconceptions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T12:07:39-07:00","id":878904,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=878835#post878835\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This looks very promising to me. Has it come up that an Iremite mummy has tried to correct your Roman's...misconceptions?</div> </div> </div> </div>I never actually got the chance to play the character, so it didn't come up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T12:19:49-07:00","id":878921,"title":"","content":"I want to see what Rio has to say on the subject. Several mummies in that setting seem to have adopted the local culture as their own, at least superficially.<br /> <br /> I remain generally of the opinion that more Storytelling and Player options is NOT a bad thing, however. Having a thematic focus within the setting is fine, but for example Changeling would have been a very different game if it had solely been about European style fairies to the exclusion of any other options like Kami or nature spirits. Shrug. I agree that Mummy: the Curse benefits from the pseudo-Egyptian focus, but I don't see the expansion into other ancient cultures as any kind of weakness depending on how it is done.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-30T12:32:30-07:00","id":878930,"title":"","content":"No one is disagreeing that the Arisen have assimilated all over the world, and often remember their adopted cultures better than they do Egypt, much less true Irem.<br /> <br /> That is a very different conversation than alternate ORIGINS for the Deathless.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T07:10:32-07:00","id":880140,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=878930#post878930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No one is disagreeing that the Arisen have assimilated all over the world, and often remember their adopted cultures better than they do Egypt, much less true Irem.<br /> <br /> That is a very different conversation than alternate ORIGINS for the Deathless.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is why suggestions like false memories and Twice Arisen are never going to be satifying to people who want mummies from other cultures than Egypt/proto-Egypt. They amount to a character who's just deluded, and one who'll never have metaphysics reflecting those cultures' religous systems and worldviews. But that's also the reason why such mummies would be better represented as completely different creatures, unrelated to the Deathless and Lifeless.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T09:46:44-07:00","id":880229,"title":"","content":"Certainly, and I'm not opposed to completely different entities existing. But they'd have to be completely different entities, not just Arisen reskinned for a different cultural origin.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T10:46:45-07:00","id":880284,"title":"","content":"OK, so the goal in making non-Iremite mummies is to have something recognizably different from the Arisen of Irem, but something similar enough that an Iremite could see them and have an uncanny valley &quot;what the Hell is that?&quot; moment. So, where do we draw the line? Just how different must &quot;completely different&quot; really be?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T16:23:30-07:00","id":880562,"title":"","content":"That's what I was trying to construct: Mummies whose origin was connected to a separate divinity which, eventually, had crumbled into the domain of Ammut. They'd have separate metaphysics but still a connection to Iremite mythology","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T19:12:01-07:00","id":880664,"title":"","content":"How is that different from a Shuankhsen?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-02T20:45:01-07:00","id":880711,"title":"","content":"Yeah, this conversation already happened","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T09:09:56-07:00","id":881033,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=880711#post880711\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, this conversation already happened</div> </div> </div> </div>Why do I feel like the Arisen now, where all conversations are mere echoes of earlier conversations?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T10:29:45-07:00","id":881080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=881033#post881033\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Why do I feel like the Arisen now, where all conversations are mere echoes of earlier conversations?</div> </div> </div> </div>Any significant amount of time on the internet'll make you an Arisen this way.<br /> <br /> Imagine listening to some of the popular arguments for ten years. I live in awe of people who have done them longer.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T10:39:26-07:00","id":881088,"title":"","content":"Lion-Headed Arisen hipster: &quot;I was chiseling cat images before they were cool&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T13:11:38-07:00","id":881203,"title":"","content":"No, the last conversation was a statement of perception ('What Shuankhsen look like to the Arisen', or some such), whereas mine was a question of being ('How is that different from a Shuankhsen?') But that's just me being pedantic.<br /> <br /> Back to the issue at hand; if these foreign mummies have had their patrons &quot;crumble in to the domain of Ammut&quot; (and what would that even entail?), then how would they differ from Ammut's Lifeless slaves? Or, since any Arisen could potentially become a Shuankhsen, is their no real difference at all, just a varying percentage of those that have been claimed by the Devourer?<br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> Let's try this: Let's break the Arisen Template into its basic components. There needs to be a Power Stat, a Fuel Stat, an Integrity Stat, and the various major and minor powers (Utterances, Merits, Affinities, etc.). Now, to keep the New!Mummies recognizably similar, I think the Power Stat should just be Sekhem by another name, complete with magics that are more or less the same as those used by the Arisen, or at least equal to them.<br /> <br /> Where the differences really kick in are with the Fuel Stat; the Arisen/Shuankhsen use the portions of the five-part soul, which ties a key part of their being to Irem's mytho-history. Following that line of thought, what sort of Fuel Stat's could be developed? Off the top of my head, I'm thinking of souls divided/defined in different ways (<u>Kindred of the East's</u> 'Hun - P'o - Yin - Yang' selection comes to mind).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T13:24:35-07:00","id":881214,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=881203#post881203\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, the last conversation was a statement of perception ('What Shuankhsen look like to the Arisen', or some such), whereas mine was a question of being ('How is that different from a Shuankhsen?') But that's just me being pedantic.<br /> <br /> Back to the issue at hand; if these foreign mummies have had their patrons &quot;crumble in to the domain of Ammut&quot; (and what would that even entail?), then how would they differ from Ammut's Lifeless slaves? Or, since any Arisen could potentially become a Shuankhsen, is their no real difference at all, just a varying percentage of those that have been claimed by the Devourer?<br /> <br /> ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------<br /> <br /> Let's try this: Let's break the Arisen Template into its basic components. There needs to be a Power Stat, a Fuel Stat, an Integrity Stat, and the various major and minor powers (Utterances, Merits, Affinities, etc.). Now, to keep the New!Mummies recognizably similar, I think the Power Stat should just be Sekhem by another name, complete with magics that are more or less the same as those used by the Arisen, or at least equal to them.<br /> <br /> Where the differences really kick in are with the Fuel Stat; the Arisen/Shuankhsen use the portions of the five-part soul, which ties a key part of their being to Irem's mytho-history. Following that line of thought, what sort of Fuel Stat's could be developed? Off the top of my head, I'm thinking of souls divided/defined in different ways (<u>Kindred of the East's</u> 'Hun - P'o - Yin - Yang' selection comes to mind).</div> </div> </div> </div>So, only being different by Pillar-equivalents and Affinities/Utterances? Bear in mind that dropping down to 4 Fuel Stats gives East!Mummies less fuel to work with. But even aside from that, <i>why</i> do they use Sekhem? Why do they awaken at 10 and descend slowly? How can they prolong their time, without Judges to serve for Descent Resets? Why do they seek Relics?<br /> <br /> You can answer &quot;they don't&quot; to any of those, but then you are diverging more and more from Mummy. At what point is it not really Mummy any more?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T17:24:36-07:00","id":881376,"title":"","content":"Well, keep in mind that this is just off the top of my head, but...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 1.<i>why</i> do they use Sekhem? </div> </div> </div>Because a rose, is a rose, is a rose. Call it Sekhem, Chi, Oteotl, Waka, or what all else, it's essentially the same thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2.Why do they awaken at 10 and descend slowly? </div> </div> </div>For the same reason batteries start full and eventually go empty.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 3.How can they prolong their time, without Judges to serve for Descent Resets? </div> </div> </div>That depends on the ur-culture they hail from / whatever patrons they might still have.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 4.Why do they seek Relics? </div> </div> </div>Again, depends on the culture/patron. Send them off to their patrons? Destroy them to release energy back into the world? Who knows?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-03T20:40:20-07:00","id":881451,"title":"","content":"I don't think a non-Iremite Mummy should seek relics. They might have a Purpose (or not) but it doesn't have to be about seeking out lost treasures or enacting vengeance.<br /> <br /> The idea I had, for example, was actually relatively Purposeless Mummies, presenting a theme that maybe freedom isn't a good thing, maybe Apotheosis isn't the answer. The truth, of course, is that these guys are Lifeless and being Purposeless <i>is</i> horrible because they're lost and aimless, their minds being malleable by anything that can supernaturally influence someone. More often than not, that means they fall under the compulsion of Ammut.<br /> <br /> It really depends if they're supposed to be playable or not. Again, I'm envisioning Lifeless antagonists. Perhaps they use Sekhem, perhaps not; I'm not worried about it, and I don't think they have Memory either. The Pillars are the only thing I'd replace, and probably not all five. Just one source of fuel","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-04T10:54:07-07:00","id":881980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=881451#post881451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It really depends if they're supposed to be playable or not. Again, I'm envisioning Lifeless antagonists. Perhaps they use Sekhem, perhaps not; I'm not worried about it, and I don't think they have Memory either. The Pillars are the only thing I'd replace, and probably not all five. Just one source of fuel</div> </div> </div> </div>That's where we part ways; I'm trying to do up playable templates for Deathless characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-05T05:39:22-07:00","id":882792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=881451#post881451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think a non-Iremite Mummy should seek relics. They might have a Purpose (or not) but it doesn't have to be about seeking out lost treasures or enacting vengeance.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mummies of the Andes were sacrifices, meant to represent their people in Hananpacha, or messengers between Hananpacha and Earth (which can be conflated easily enough) - others were meant to impart strength and guidance to their communities, which could be considered tribal cults in Mummy the Curse terms. As purposes, those are close enough to the Deathless experience for some commonality. I'd suggest they're called up by their people to deal with problems, and have a sense similar to Khepera, applying to seeking out solutions rather than relics (interpreted as wisdom from the gods). Apotheosis wouldn't be a goal to such mummies, but a salve for those whose communities are gone. I'd tone their power down too - analogues of Affinities, but not Utterances - and maybe the ability to empower their people in some way (maybe burning Huaca to do it).<br /> <br /> It would be easy for the Arisen to conclude that such mummies were made via some version of the Rite of Return, more successful than other copies. Only whatever spirituality they're connected to doesn't appear to be the Judges. And they could just as easily be wrong.<br /> <br /> I'd change the Pillars in this case, though I'm not certain how. I'd give them a trait analogous to Sekhem, which would be able to do most of the same things.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-10T19:48:27-07:00","id":887767,"title":"","content":"I believe we could have a book about Mummies originated from three cultures. Andean, China and Ethiopia. Specific Gods for these mummies, two guilds for each of these mummies, said that there was a copy of the nameless rite is established equivalent to the three types of the five parts of the soul. They put everything in a book and ready. In addition to its own relics, judges and tradition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":641,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/641-ben-linus","user":"Ben Linus","date":". 05-10-2016, 10:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/641-ben-linus","name":"Ben Linus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-10T20:10:07-07:00","id":887777,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ben Linus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=887767#post887767\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I believe we could have a book about Mummies originated from three cultures. Andean, China and Ethiopia. Specific Gods for these mummies, two guilds for each of these mummies, said that there was a copy of the nameless rite is established equivalent to the three types of the five parts of the soul. They put everything in a book and ready. In addition to its own relics, judges and tradition.</div> </div> </div> </div>We don't need a retread of Mummy: the Resurrection. MtC is the story of Irem; just let it be that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-10T20:32:24-07:00","id":887792,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=887777#post887777\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> We don't need a retread of Mummy: the Resurrection. MtC is the story of Irem; just let it be that.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> There is a retread. There is room for other people to have copied Return Rite, which prepares the way for something for the mummies that ascend or almost amount to judges. In the rules and record would be the same, would soon be almost like playing with the Requiem with Dead like Wicked with Kindred as Pijavica.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/641-ben-linus","name":"Ben Linus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T00:57:04-07:00","id":887969,"title":"","content":"<b>Wicked Dead</b> is absolutely what inspires me the most in regards to the possibilities of what we might be able to do. I don't think we have to go full-blown Chinese/Andean analogues, but I think I'm the forumite that's pushing the conversation hardest for non-Iremite Mummies that are Lifeless rather than something 'playable' so I might be biased","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T02:06:07-07:00","id":887996,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ben Linus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=887792#post887792\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There is a retread. There is room for other people to have copied Return Rite, which prepares the way for something for the mummies that ascend or almost amount to judges. In the rules and record would be the same, would soon be almost like playing with the Requiem with Dead like Wicked with Kindred as Pijavica.</div> </div> </div> </div>Things like Pijavica work in Requiem because a) the origins of vampirism are unknown in VtR, and b) there are different legends of the hungry dead from all over the world that despite all their differences bear enough similarities to each other that running them with a slightly tweaked vampire template makes sense.<br /> <br /> The same cannot be said of MtC. Origins of the Arisen are well-detailed, and a lot of game considerations stem from their specificity. More importantly, while many civilizations throughout the ages practiced mummification, only one of them gave rise to legends of mummies as undead monsters hurling horrible curses at those who disturb the peace of their tombs. The Arisen are modeled after a mixture of Universal horror movies and pseudo-Egyptian mysticism in a way that permeates every aspect of the game, from aesthetics to mechanics to story considerations.<br /> <br /> There's a place in Chronicles of Darkness for many other types of immortal/undead beings, be they Andean mummies or Taoist immortals or Sokushinbutsu or what have you. It's just that Mummy: the Curse is not the game to model their mechanics after. Why would a Chinese Immortal suffer through Descent cycles? Why would a Sokushinbutsu rise from his meditation to recover relics? Trying to shoehorn such beings into the Arisen template makes them look like cheap knockoffs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T05:21:51-07:00","id":888082,"title":"","content":"That's all very true, and I'm <i>glad</i> <b>Mummy the Curse</b> focuses on Egypt because, like you said, that's where the horror mythology of mummies comes from.<br /> <br /> Still, the core book does indeed inspire the possibilities:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mummies exist in many cultures, each with a distinctive form of magic that brings it to life.<br /> <br /> False, <b>as far as the Arisen know</b>. Only the sorcerer-priests of Irem knew the Rite of Return, and only they cast it upon their subjects. Even if other cultures could somehow use effective sorcery to raise their blessed dead, such magics are pale reflections of the eldritch Rite of Return. </div> </div> </div>All <i>I </i>want are Lifeless <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":638,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","user":"reseru","date":". 05-11-2016, 05:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T06:44:50-07:00","id":888131,"title":"","content":"Well, pretty much any dead body that rises through magic qualifies as Lifeless, so there's that :P<br /> <br /> In all seriousness, though, I'm not trying to discourage anyone. In fact, I'm quite interested in anything people might come up with, because when it comes to Chronicles, I have the &quot;the more the merrier&quot; approach and am never shy to dig up some obscure critter for my games just to mess with my players and their futile attempts to categorize the world into neat little boxes <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> In fact, part of the reason for my rant is this desire to not have things neatly categorized. I really hated it when oWoD Mummy introduced Taoist Immortals as the same kind of being as the Amenti, it felt extremely shoehorned to me, a wasted opportunity to shake things up and expand the cosmology.<br /> <br /> To go back to the Vampire comparison, MtC is more like Masquerade than Requiem, in that it has a very specific origin story for its protagonists. Due to that, I'd prefer it if non-Iremite mummies were handled more like Kindred of the East were handled in Masquerade than like Asian domains are handled in Requiem. Something different, something that gives the Arisen pause when encountered, something bearing certain similarities but recognizably different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T06:57:51-07:00","id":888142,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=888082#post888082\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All <i>I</i> want are Lifeless <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't mind <b>Deathless</b>, provided it gets some not-synonymous-with-Arisen/Deceived definition. I'm fine with wildly different systems, since they'd have different to wildly different origins, so long as we can unpack and look at the definitions of Deathless and Lifeless.<br /> <br /> I'd say it is the Sekhem (being kinda Life Energy), but Shuankhsen have Sekhem too...<br /> <br /> If it <b>was</b> Sekhem, there should be more uses/manifestations of it, since we know it occurs &quot;in nature&quot;, can be measured, and can be transferred/manipulated. I'd be fine if other sources weren't nearly as skilled with it without the Shan'iatu's guidance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 05-11-2016, 10:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-11T07:39:31-07:00","id":888185,"title":"","content":"You could crib Beast's Satiety mechanics and refer to it literally as a Sekhem battery: basically, the non-Arisen mummies are the product of early scientists/naturalists/occultists who encountered Sekhem, and found some way to empower themselves or a target in a sacrificial rite. These non-Arisen wouldn't necessarily start at Power Stat 10: they'd be more like Kindred or Begotten, and one of the reasons they'd seek out relics is because they can't regenerate Sekhem themselves like an Arisen.<br /> <br /> Instead of the Five-Pillar Soul model, they could have a more traditional pool of Fuel Stat that they use to charge their powers, or just use Willpower points (though this makes them more like a minor template). Alternatively, you could use Sekhem as both a Power <i>and</i> Fuel Stat.<br /> <br /> For the record, I'm wholly against Arisen being anything but proto-Egyptian: it's not just boring, but borderline racist since it says, &quot;All cultures that preserved corpses in a ritualistic fashion are basically interchangeable,&quot; and saying something like, &quot;But we've renamed the mechanics&quot; is <i>not</i> good enough to differentiate them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-12T16:34:26-07:00","id":889451,"title":"","content":"&quot;You could crib Beast's Satiety mechanics and refer to it literally as a Sekhem battery: basically, the non-Arisen mummies are the product of early scientists/naturalists/occultists who encountered Sekhem, and found some way to empower themselves or a target in a sacrificial rite. These non-Arisen wouldn't necessarily start at Power Stat 10: they'd be more like Kindred or Begotten, and one of the reasons they'd seek out relics is because they can't regenerate Sekhem themselves like an Arisen.&quot;<br /> <br /> Certainly workable.<br /> <br /> &quot;Instead of the Five-Pillar Soul model, they could have a more traditional pool of Fuel Stat that they use to charge their powers, or just use Willpower points (though this makes them more like a minor template). Alternatively, you could use Sekhem as both a Power <i>and</i> Fuel Stat.&quot;<br /> <br /> Meh. We should keep the terminology the game left us with and adapt it to the situation. If the mummy is going to have some access to Arisen abilities it will need some equivalent of Arisen stats. Better to tweak the existing concept than to go Changeling: The Dreaming here and create an entirely new entity that vaguely resembles your original game creatures for any given region of the globe. <br /> <br /> &quot;For the record, I'm wholly against Arisen being anything but proto-Egyptian:&quot;<br /> <br /> I continue to disagree with this view. Diversity in playable options and ST concept fodder isn't a bad thing and keeps the game from getting stale.<br /> <br /> &quot;it's not just boring&quot; <br /> <br /> This statement floors me. If diversity in a monstrous concept is boring then White Wolf done fucked up since the very beginning! Vampires should only ever be from Transylvania, werewolves should only ever be victims of a cursed bite and uncontrollable transformations, etc. Its true that Mummy has gotten a surprising amount of mileage out of the Egyptian-esque (blended with a more modern Gothic/Lovecraftian horror asthetic, of course). But that simple fact right there says it all, really. There isn't a single reason that you could paint as rich and atmospheric a tapestry using the culture and mythology of any ancient culture on the Earth as this game has already done with the Egyptian culture. Yes, that's probably too ambitious a thing to ask for, but I suppose that's what I'm getting at here. If you are willing to put the effort and research into the project I see no reason whatsoever a mummy from say Scandinavia or Central America would be an inch less interesting than one from Egypt. Also, less sterotypical<br /> <br /> &quot;but borderline racist since it says, &quot;All cultures that preserved corpses in a ritualistic fashion are basically interchangeable,&quot; and saying something like, &quot;But we've renamed the mechanics&quot; is <i>not</i> good enough to differentiate them.&quot;<br /> <br /> .......<br /> .......<br /> ........<br /> <br /> O.........kay. So....mummies arising in various societies from around the world. BAD AND RACIST! But mummies arising solely in this one tiny region of the world because their magic was just so much more awesome and their gods so much more real than everyone else that they are the only ones who could ever pull it off. GOOD AND RACIALLY PROGRESSIVE! <br /> <br /> Nope. Sorry. Does not fucking compute. Maybe I'm just still stuck in Changeling: The Lost mode here, but I do not consider a game that allows you to make a character based around a few quintessential core mechanics and a lot of open ended options you can base on the vast of array of myths and societies we have available in this wide world a bad thing. <br /> <br /> I dunno. Much as I love the specifics of the setting, maybe second edition should include a section (in storytelling or antagonists) that helps an ST &quot;decouple&quot; the core mechanics from the Egyptian motif in order to create a more unique antagonist. Preferably in a way that forces the PCs to do what your normal mortal protagonists have to do in a good mummy horror movie- learn enough of the entity's history and culture to be able to combat it effectively. In fact, I would argue that's the best reason to include a non-iremite mummy right there- it keeps them guessing. Here's an entity similar enough to them to be frightening (like, say, not having the courtesy to stay dead when it gets put down) but different enough that the usual tricks just aren't enough. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-13T05:11:18-07:00","id":889799,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=889451#post889451\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">O.........kay. So....mummies arising in various societies from around the world. BAD AND RACIST! But mummies arising solely in this one tiny region of the world because their magic was just so much more awesome and their gods so much more real than everyone else that they are the only ones who could ever pull it off. GOOD AND RACIALLY PROGRESSIVE!</div> </div> </div> </div>The actual point was more along the lines of: The Arisen are (proto-)Egyptian mummies specifically. If you want non-Egyptian mummies, make them from scratch instead of trying to shoehorn them into either the mechanical template or the cosmology of MtC, because doing that would be insensitive towards all those cultures that preserved the bodies of their deceased through completely different methods stemming from completely different cosmologies and belief systems and aimed towards completely different goals than the Egyptians did.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-13T06:28:38-07:00","id":889837,"title":"","content":"I agree, Morangias. In the example I pitched here, though, I was thinking of a way to do that while still making them part of <b>Mummy</b>, rather than the 'non-Iremite Mummies' of <b>Vampire</b>, <b>Promethean</b>, or Immortals. The compromise I considered there was that while they were created wholly separate and apart from the Deathless, in later centuries their divine source was Devoured.<br /> <br /> Possibly the most insensitive of all, but how many faiths have been buried under the sands of time anyway","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-13T07:20:08-07:00","id":889865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=889799#post889799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The actual point was more along the lines of: The Arisen are (proto-)Egyptian mummies specifically. If you want non-Egyptian mummies, make them from scratch instead of trying to shoehorn them into either the mechanical template or the cosmology of MtC, because doing that would be insensitive towards all those cultures that preserved the bodies of their deceased through completely different methods stemming from completely different cosmologies and belief systems and aimed towards completely different goals than the Egyptians did.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly this was my point: don't just reskin the Judges as the Celestial Bureaucracy and say that now you've got Chinese Mummies. <i>That</i> would be racist. Feel free to use something else: say a group of powerful Shen resurrect the bodies of properly-prepared corpses. Chinese mummies! Basically yeah, don't make mummies from different cultures interchangeable.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/127887-doing-first-tongue-translations?p=484226#post484226\" target=\"_blank\">Nihhina kalekal-zidu kal masun, kal manudanadu. Nihhina kalekal-zidu nukal shaghu-desasudu \u2014 nihhina kalekal-zidu kal innu-desasudu udhkal samm</a>.&quot; Arthur Ashe<br /> She/her, contributing writer for <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/363404/Scion-Pride-2021\" target=\"_blank\">Scion: Pride 2021</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1923-bigdamnhero","name":"BigDamnHero","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1923_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1263"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-13T08:22:44-07:00","id":889914,"title":"","content":"What's the benefit of this compromise, the purpose of this connection? I'm not trying to be flippant or condescending here, I'm genuinely wondering. Since the main purpose of introducing character types other than Arisen is so that PCs have something to contrast themselves with, and since we already have two other types of mummies tied to Irem, its gods and the Rite, wouldn't any further additions to the roster serve this purpose better if they were even further removed from the experience of the Arisen? Since confronting the legacy of Irem (either by upholding it or rebelling against it) is such an important theme to any Mummy game, wouldn't it shake things up more to confront players with something superficially similar but not tied to Irem in any way? What would the Arisen do upon learning there are dwellings of the gods other than Duat, beings that grant immortality beside the Judges? Could this be true, or are these just some Lifeless abominations with delusions of grandeur? How will the Judges react to the Arisen who choose to interact peacefully with such beings?<br /> <br /> EDIT: I was writing this in response to reseru's post, but my Internet went bonkers so it didn't register before BigDamnHero's response.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":582,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","user":"Morangias","date":". 05-13-2016, 11:59 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-13T13:20:20-07:00","id":890129,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=889799#post889799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The actual point was more along the lines of: The Arisen are (proto-)Egyptian mummies specifically. If you want non-Egyptian mummies, make them from scratch instead of trying to shoehorn them into either the mechanical template or the cosmology of MtC, because doing that would be insensitive towards all those cultures that preserved the bodies of their deceased through completely different methods stemming from completely different cosmologies and belief systems and aimed towards completely different goals than the Egyptians did.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, yeah, yeah. I understand the argument perfectly well. I just don't see the need for this level of caution. Okay, so you want to show respect of an ancient civilization. Great! Maybe you want to also raise awareness about their history and traditions and offer up a compelling and accurate depiction of their society. I think that's a fantastic goal! But beyond that and an effort to weave an immersive story the way its already been done with the Egyptian theme, I think we're being too timid when we're talking about avoiding adapting the existing template to other regions. For one thing, the racism issue...people, we're already sorta pissing on Egyptian civilization with this game. Their god of the storm and divine adversary to Osiris is treated like a hero, their Judges of Order and Law are self-serving monsters, and generally the goal was to ensure that the departed made it to Osiris (or whoever the favorite deity was at the time) and have a peaceful afterlife....NOT recalling them to perform menial work thousands of years later. Several aspects of this here game are all modern elements we attached to mummies in our stories and films. I am going to argue vehemently that a MUMMY (now speaking of the horror icon) has a few key aspects to its nature that make it what it is. I believe our goal should be to define those key aspects (including the core game mechanics we're using to play Iremite mummies right now) and continue to tie those into any new Mummy templates we design. It will save all kinds of headaches and allow the authors to focus on other asthetics to help make the non-iremites interesting and unique. <br /> <br /> Or to put it simply- I don't think a simple &quot;rename everything&quot; approach is acceptable either....but I'm okay if what we deem to be the most essential parts of what makes a Mummy a Mummy are largely untouched. I think right now we have the makings of an excellent set of &quot;bare bones&quot; for a Mummy PC here. I think it is possible to strip away the Egyptian elements and create a new creature (by all means, with a purely unique culture, gods, afterlife, whatever) but still keep the mechanics familiar enough not to disenfranchise the players and STs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-14T00:09:32-07:00","id":890469,"title":"","content":"Here's the thing: not only is mummy the horror icon firmly Egyptian in origins, the actual MtC template is very much its own thing, cribbing the aesthetics from the classic horror movies (somewhat) and the Brendan Fraser movies (mostly) but changing the origins almost completely to make them a reasonable protagonist choice in the context of World (Chronicles) of Darkness.<br /> <br /> The only commonality the Arisen have with mummy the horror icon are the Egyptian aesthetics and the biblical scale of their magic, the latter cribbed firmly from the Fraser movies.<br /> <br /> MtC tells a very specific story: you were a favored servant of dread necromancers from before Egypt was known as such, remade into an eternal servant of their horrible death gods.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, the only commonality between all the other things we call mummies is that they're preserved corpses - that's literally the extent of it. The method of preservation varies from place to place, the reasons for the preservation vary from accidental to elaborately deliberate, the status of those preserved varies from criminals to ritual sacrifices to priests to kings to saints to random people who happened to be buried in the right place for their bodies to dry up rather than rot.<br /> <br /> Frankly, there's no common ground to tie them all into the same template, even if the template was more generic than it was. Mummies as undead aren't a common belief of the cultures that mummified their dead - they are what European culture gave us over several bouts of Egyptomania. Frankly, it's kinda the opposite - elaborate burial rites were invented to placate the dead and make sure they <i>don't</i> rise up to bother the living.<br /> <br /> So, what I'm saying is that there is no generic mummy template the same way there arguably is a generic vampire template or a generic fae template. Trying to make one is misguided, trying to model it after the ultra-specific systems of MtC is plain wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-14T03:27:33-07:00","id":890529,"title":"","content":"Really, Mummy the horror icon is pretty firmly British in origins. Like you said, the whole thing is less about actual Egyptian culture, religion and folklore and more about Egypt as being mysterious and exotic and mystical. It's kind of like a less racist version of the magical Chinese people that tended to show up in works from around the same period and somehow managed to endure far longer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4968-mrbreaksit","name":"MrBreaksIt","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"130"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-14T05:45:41-07:00","id":890550,"title":"","content":"It's kinda ironic, really - one of the oldest monsters in Chronicles of Darkness are some of the youngest when it comes to RL inspirations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-14T11:28:41-07:00","id":890670,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=887969#post887969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Wicked Dead</b> is absolutely what inspires me the most in regards to the possibilities of what we might be able to do. I don't think we have to go full-blown Chinese/Andean analogues, but I think I'm the forumite that's pushing the conversation hardest for non-Iremite Mummies that are Lifeless rather than something 'playable' so I might be biased</div> </div> </div> </div>Wicked Dead made its entities playable, there's no reason a Mummy analogue couldn't do likewise. What it didn't was make them (necessarily) Kindred - it just borrowed mechanics, because they were convenient for representing that sort of entity - again, a Mummy analogue should do likewise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-16T16:34:40-07:00","id":892108,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=890670#post890670\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Wicked Dead made its entities playable, there's no reason a Mummy analogue couldn't do likewise. What it didn't was make them (necessarily) Kindred - it just borrowed mechanics, because they were convenient for representing that sort of entity - again, a Mummy analogue should do likewise.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but the mechanics Wicked Dead was cribbing from aren't culturally specific in origin. What we're trying to get at is that there is no connection between Egyptian Mummies and, say, Incan Mummies. Zip. None. The mechanics for Vampire or Promethean (or even better, Horrors or Revenants as the most generic template for undead) are equally valid for representing an Incan Mummy. You can <i>easily</i> argue that they are actually more so, because if you borrow from Mummy then you need to do a load of additional work removing elements like the five Egyptian Pillars of the soul. Literally the only reason you are even suggesting using Mummy's mechanics is because the in English we call any preserved corpse a &quot;mummy&quot;. These corpses have no similarities beyond some element of arrested decomposition.<br /> <br /> The question that always gets posed when Non-Iremite Mummies come up is &quot;Sure, but why use Mummy for mechanics?&quot; I don't think I've ever heard a response good enough to explain what makes Mummy the better base template than any other form of undead.<br /> <br /> Actually, I'll go one step further. Mummy: The Curse gives you the rules to play a non-Iremite Mummy. Create a Lucid Dead Ghost. Make it a Revenant. Stat as appropriate from there. Add some fluff so that it isn't &quot;just&quot; a ghost possessing a corpse, but all your mechanics for generic undead of every variety are right there in the Mummy core book. No reworking of culturally tied mechanics necessary.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-17T07:02:22-07:00","id":892468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=892108#post892108\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What we're trying to get at is that there is no connection between Egyptian Mummies and, say,Incan<br /> <br /> Mummies. Zip. None.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is why I've said over and over and over that Mallqui, since that's your example, would best be represented as completely different creatures from either Deathless or Lifeless (even to the point of wishing for a separate line). I'm only talking about it herebecase the thread discussing it is here, and I'm only referring to Wicked Dead because the subject has come up. You're arguing at the wrong person.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Literally the only reason you are even suggesting using Mummy's mechanics is because the in English we call any preserved corpse a &quot;mummy&quot;. </div> </div> </div>Right, because I couldn't possibly think the Sahu mechanics are a good way of representing undead bodies, or that the cult mechanics are a good way of representing the ties to community that would be a big deal for Andean mummies, or that the Khepera mechanics are a good way of representing occult senses, or that the Sybaris mechanics are a good way of representing the weight of immortal power on mortals, or that the Descent mechanics are a good start for representing beings who are messengers between heaven and earth or ancestors invoked to help in times of crisis (both considerations for Andean mummies), or that Affinities are a good way to contrast ancient power with human hearts - none of those are cultureally specific either beyond using Egyptian names. I couldn't possibly have actually <i>thought</i> about the subject.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Actually, I'll go one step further. Mummy: The Curse gives you the rules to play a non-Iremite Mummy. Create a Lucid Dead Ghost. Make it a Revenant. Stat as appropriate from there. Add some fluff so that it isn't &quot;just&quot; a ghost possessing a corpse, but all your mechanics for generic undead of every variety are right there in the Mummy core book. No reworking of culturally tied mechanics necessary. </div> </div> </div>Not bad. I'd adapt the Sahu mechanics to cover when and how well the revenant body can look human, and maybe something kind of like Affinities in place of Numina. Keep Age as their power stat rather than some version of Sekhem. And give them a fuel stat of some sort in lieu of Pillars or Essence. I could work with that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":321,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","user":"SunlessNick","date":". 05-17-2016, 12:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-17T14:40:10-07:00","id":892787,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=892468#post892468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which is why I've said over and over and over that Mallqui, since that's your example, would best be represented as completely different creatures from either Deathless or Lifeless (even to the point of wishing for a separate line). I'm only talking about it herebecase the thread discussing it is here, and I'm only referring to Wicked Dead because the subject has come up. You're arguing at the wrong person.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair enough, but I do disagree with your stance that a Wicked Dead analogue to Mummy should present playable Mummies from different cultures for all of the reasons I listed.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=892468#post892468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right, because I couldn't possibly think the Sahu mechanics are a good way of representing undead bodies, or that the cult mechanics are a good way of representing the ties to community that would be a big deal for Andean mummies, or that the Khepera mechanics are a good way of representing occult senses, or that the Sybaris mechanics are a good way of representing the weight of immortal power on mortals, or that the Descent mechanics are a good start for representing beings who are messengers between heaven and earth or ancestors invoked to help in times of crisis (both considerations for Andean mummies), or that Affinities are a good way to contrast ancient power with human hearts - none of those are cultureally specific either beyond using Egyptian names. I couldn't possibly have actually <i>thought</i> about the subject.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Sahu mechanic is hardly unique in the World of Darkness. Many supernatural beings have some equivalent. Cult mechanics are similar, in that you can find some version of them in many different lines. If you think the Mummy interpretation is the best one, fair enough.<br /> <br /> The others are getting a little strange though. Why would a non-Arisen being with no relation to the line have Kepher, even under another name? What requires such a creature to have an equivalent innate method of searching for the occult? It's an odd overlap for something of a totally different line, but it just gets worse as you begin to pile on Sybaris and the Descent. You are rapidly ending up with a creature that is Arisen in all but name, hardly unique enough to warrant its own line and almost identical thematically. Precisely the kind of idea that so many people here are vehemently against.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=892468#post892468\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not bad. I'd adapt the Sahu mechanics to cover when and how well the revenant body can look human, and maybe something kind of like Affinities in place of Numina. Keep Age as their power stat rather than some version of Sekhem. And give them a fuel stat of some sort in lieu of Pillars or Essence. I could work with that.</div> </div> </div> </div>You could also port in some equivalent of the Mortal Mask Numen from second edition (is there a first edition version? I can't recall).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-18T08:29:02-07:00","id":893335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=892787#post892787\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do disagree with your stance that a Wicked Dead analogue to Mummy should present playable Mummies from different cultures for all of the reasons I listed.</div> </div> </div> </div>My actual stance is that <i>if</i> it includes similar beings to Deathless from elsewhere in the world, <i>then</i> it might as well make them playable. As I said, you're arguing at the wrong person.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You are rapidly ending up with a creature that is Arisen in all but name, hardly unique enough to warrant its own line and almost identical thematically. Precisely the kind of idea that so many people here are vehemently against. </div> </div> </div>Yes the things that make Andean mummies interesting to me do overlap quite a bit with the Deathless &quot;lifestyle&quot; - which is in fact <i>why</i>, in the absence of sufficient assed points to make a full homebrew from scratch, I'd crib a lot of Curse mechanics - to raise the question, even if the answer is no, as to whether they're connected in any way (and because Curse has the most a collection of mechanics to draw from). If I was trying to make a whole fansplat from them, I'd broaden them beyond my specific interest in them.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, the things that make, say, the hardcore Buddhist monk mummies from the Himalayas or Japan, have nothing in common with the Deathless and I'd probably start with Purified (or even end with Purified, since I suspect said mummies were one of the main inspirations for it).<br /> <br /> The things that would interest me about using a bog body or Central Asian ice grave is essentially the same thing that would interest me about a ghost; those I'd just make revenants, albeit very powerful ones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You could also port in some equivalent of the Mortal Mask Numen from second edition (is there a first edition version? I can't recall). </div> </div> </div>Or make their living semblance dependent on the strength of their ties to communities.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Anyway, this conversation is sapping my will to live, so I'm going to bail, I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":321,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","user":"SunlessNick","date":". 05-18-2016, 11:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-18T08:59:11-07:00","id":893358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=893335#post893335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes the things that make Andean mummies interesting to me do overlap quite a bit with the Deathless &quot;lifestyle&quot; - which is in fact <i>why</i>, in the absence of sufficient assed points to make a full homebrew from scratch, I'd crib a lot of Curse mechanics - to raise the question, even if the answer is no, as to whether they're connected in any way (and because Curse has the most a collection of mechanics to draw from).</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem I have with this is that them actually being connected would feel cheap, while them not being connected yet ending up similar would feel weird, and not in a good, interesting way. It'd be like Promethean dropped the common &quot;power source&quot; of Divine Fire, but all Created ended up with the same template for no reason.<br /> <br /> The other problem I have with Andean mummies being made into undead creatures is that they were <i>ritually murdered children</i>, and that's not something I'm personally comfortable with as part of my horror entertainment. But that's probably just me.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-18T14:59:08-07:00","id":893639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=893335#post893335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My actual stance is that <i>if</i> it includes similar beings to Deathless from elsewhere in the world, <i>then</i> it might as well make them playable. As I said, you're arguing at the wrong person.</div> </div> </div> </div>I dunno, you keep saying that and then saying things I disagree with <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=893335#post893335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes the things that make Andean mummies interesting to me do overlap quite a bit with the Deathless &quot;lifestyle&quot; - which is in fact <i>why</i>, in the absence of sufficient assed points to make a full homebrew from scratch, I'd crib a lot of Curse mechanics - to raise the question, even if the answer is no, as to whether they're connected in any way (and because Curse has the most a collection of mechanics to draw from). If I was trying to make a whole fansplat from them, I'd broaden them beyond my specific interest in them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Morangias's point on this is a valid one. I'd also add that raising the question of a relationship is odd because of the aforementioned &quot;Egyptian Mummies and Incan Mummies are not in any way related&quot; aspect. It would make as much sense as designing your Vampire line to similarly raise questions of a relationship through related mechanics. It's starting to feel closer to Old World of Darkness than anything in Chronicles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=893335#post893335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On the other hand, the things that make, say, the hardcore Buddhist monk mummies from the Himalayas or Japan, have nothing in common with the Deathless and I'd probably start with Purified (or even end with Purified, since I suspect said mummies were one of the main inspirations for it).<br /> <br /> The things that would interest me about using a bog body or Central Asian ice grave is essentially the same thing that would interest me about a ghost; those I'd just make revenants, albeit very powerful ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I can agree with all of those ones. I'm not sure why the Incan Mummies are special in this respect though.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=893335#post893335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or make their living semblance dependent on the strength of their ties to communities.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hm. The Arisen sort of straddle the line between representation and inversion of their base culture's approach to mummification. But I guess the Incan Mummies are a whole lot younger, so there's probably not as much time for the perversion and distortion of burial rituals described as having occurred in Egypt between the time of Irem and the rise of successor civilisations.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=893335#post893335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyway, this conversation is sapping my will to live, so I'm going to bail, I think.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe you'll Rise again? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-18T15:02:55-07:00","id":893645,"title":"","content":"I would add that thus far my favourite interpretation of Incan Mummies has been a suggested Dark Era where the Incan Mummies were an attempt at large scale creation of Twice Arisen, using the bodies of children as new vessels. Given the risks, this would have led to a very large number of Mummies becoming Shuankhsen, ravenous creatures wearing the shells of children turning the Incan Empire into one of the most bitter losses the Arisen Diaspora have ever experienced.<br /> <br /> There's something elementally disturbing about giant-mawed children with frozen blue skin haunting mountain tops and preying on other &quot;noble&quot; undead children.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-19T00:36:46-07:00","id":893885,"title":"","content":"Counter-intuitively, the skin of frost mummies tends to have a rather warm hue, ranging from yellow to dark orange.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-29T14:39:06-07:00","id":903017,"title":"","content":"Hmm. Assuming that we use the Arisen template as a starting point, what elements would absolutely need to be changed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-30T00:18:40-07:00","id":903316,"title":"","content":"Decrees and Pillars, possibly Memory, and maybe Utterances","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-30T01:58:19-07:00","id":903342,"title":"","content":"This all goes back to the Arisen being a very specific &quot;template&quot; shaped by the specific cosmology of Irem and the needs of the Shan'iatu:<br /> <br /> Pillars stem from an Egyptian view of the soul, or at least a bastardization of it, and as such make no sense for non-Iremite/Egyptian mummies.<br /> <br /> The Decrees are dependent on Pillars, and tie directly to the Arisen serving the Judges of Duat, i.e. one of the core conflicts of the game. They make no sense outside the story of the Arisen.<br /> <br /> Memory is the result of the Shan'iatu attempting to make perfect slaves devoid of free will - any culture less insanely evil than the death-obsessed empire ruled by demigod necromancers scheming against their Lovecraftian gods is unlikely to consider this a desirable outcome.<br /> <br /> The Descent cycle (i.e. waking up with Power Stat 10 and dropping successively until you have to die again) is also part of making the Arisen perfect servants for the Shan'iatu and the Judges - it ensures their undead terminators don't linger on too long after performing the desired task and since the &quot;mindless servant&quot; part of the plan had failed, it serves the Judges as an effective leash to keep the Arisen in check - note that this is completely inconvenient for everyone involved and vastly reduces the influence your undead champion can exert over the world, and again, unless the power creating these hypothetical non-Iremite mummies is any less a horrible paranoid control freak than the Judges, they're not likely to consider this a feature.<br /> <br /> Utterances have an Old Testament feel to them, and thus would feel weird in the hand of a Mezo-/Southern-American mummy or a Taoist immortal or what have you.<br /> <br /> Affinities are a bit more generic in feel and visuals, but some of them use a rather specific interpretation of Fate as an explanation, and these may not mesh well with other cultures' views on destiny and fate.<br /> <br /> Kepher is ultra-specific to the Arisen mission by its very nature.<br /> <br /> Seriously, the Arisen were created for a specific reason, and their mechanics tell a very specific story. For every specific system they use, the question is either &quot;why would a monster created for a different reason have to deal with this&quot; or &quot;what is this clearly foreign concept doing on this creature's character sheet at all&quot;.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-30T08:43:54-07:00","id":903570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=903342#post903342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pillars stem from an Egyptian view of the soul, or at least a bastardization of it, and as such make no sense for non-Iremite/Egyptian mummies.<br /> <br /> The Decrees are dependent on Pillars, and tie directly to the Arisen serving the Judges of Duat, i.e. one of the core conflicts of the game. They make no sense outside the story of the Arisen.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed and agreed. You could come up with another breakdown based on another worldview on the breakdown of the soul, but if you're doing said worldview justice, you won't get anything that matches up to the pillars. And it would probably be overkill.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Memory is the result of the Shan'iatu attempting to make perfect slaves devoid of free will - any culture less insanely evil than the death-obsessed empire ruled by demigod necromancers scheming against their Lovecraftian gods is unlikely to consider this a desirable outcome. </div> </div> </div>Also agreed. Furthermore, part of the point of doing a mummy-analogue from another culture is likely to be a desire to make it <i>belong</i> their (that's why delusional twice-born is never going to be a universally satisfying answer) - especially in the case of Andean mummies, from my own perspective, as what I'd want from them is ties to the descendants of the communities they knew in life (while the Deathless might have cults, their cults do not hail from the people of Irem) - memory issues create mummies who totally don't belong. As another consequence, any such mummy analogues are likely to be a lot younger than the Deathless.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Descent cycle (i.e. waking up with Power Stat 10 and dropping successively until you have to die again) is also part of making the Arisen perfect servants for the Shan'iatu and the Judges - it ensures their undead terminators don't linger on too long after performing the desired task and since the &quot;mindless servant&quot; part of the plan had failed, it serves the Judges as an effective leash to keep the Arisen in check - note that this is completely inconvenient for everyone involved and vastly reduces the influence your undead champion can exert over the world, and again, unless the power creating these hypothetical non-Iremite mummies is any less a horrible paranoid control freak than the Judges, they're not likely to consider this a feature. </div> </div> </div>There's another interpretation here. There are arguments to be made that had the mindless servant part of the plan succeeded, having the Deathless active indefinitely would have been desirable. Also, if a given mummy <i>is</i> acting like a perfect servant, the Judges can slow the Decent down, but not reverse it. This implies that the Descent pattern may actually be most that can be achieved, (this is part of the reason I would advocate giving it to any mummy analogues that share something of the &quot;guardian who deals with their people's problems&quot; lifestyle), and sleep is necessary to recharge.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Utterances have an Old Testament feel to them, and thus would feel weird in the hand of a Mezo-/Southern-American mummy or a Taoist immortal or what have you. </div> </div> </div>Agreed. They're also hella powerful, and any other mummy analogues should be less so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Affinities are a bit more generic in feel and visuals, but some of them use a rather specific interpretation of Fate as an explanation, and these may not mesh well with other cultures' views on destiny and fate. </div> </div> </div>Specific Affinities might - and are also tied to the Decrees - but the general pattern of how Affinities work is very much &quot;epic but human,&quot; which is likely to be a good starting point for the themes of any mummy-based critter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Kepher is ultra-specific to the Arisen mission by its very nature. </div> </div> </div>Kepher hunting for relics is ultra specific (and also, while you don't mention it, the types of relic that are relevant are based on either Egyptian art or legends about Egypt). However, if you do have a mummy analogue whose lifestyle involves solving specific problems, then Kepher works very well as a source on insight into what's relevant to said problem.<br /> <br /> Sybaris is a heavy dose of the power of the past over the present, which is likely to be a big feature of any mummy-based suprnatural.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-30T09:29:20-07:00","id":903606,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=903570#post903570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's another interpretation here. There are arguments to be made that had the mindless servant part of the plan succeeded, having the Deathless active indefinitely would have been desirable. Also, if a given mummy <i>is</i> acting like a perfect servant, the Judges can slow the Decent down, but not reverse it. This implies that the Descent pattern may actually be most that can be achieved, (this is part of the reason I would advocate giving it to any mummy analogues that share something of the &quot;guardian who deals with their people's problems&quot; lifestyle), and sleep is necessary to recharge.<br /> <br /> (...)<br /> <br /> Kepher hunting for relics is ultra specific (and also, while you don't mention it, the types of relic that are relevant are based on either Egyptian art or legends about Egypt). However, if you do have a mummy analogue whose lifestyle involves solving specific problems, then Kepher works very well as a source on insight into what's relevant to said problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>This, to me, is the big problem - why would any other mummy-like monster share this extremely specific and generally inefficient model of task-based existence? This level of &quot;evolutionary convergence&quot; would strike me as odd, especially seeing how the very idea of using the dead as lackeys is contrary to the reason any culture ever has produced mummies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sybaris is a heavy dose of the power of the past over the present, which is likely to be a big feature of any mummy-based suprnatural. </div> </div> </div>I'd contest that, considering how much older the Arisen are from anything else that could be made into a mummy-like undead. The most known Incan ice mummies are dated to 15th century, the mummies of Incan and Aztec rulers are of similar age, and only one of the Eight Immortals of Taoism is reputed to have been born before Christ. Unless you wanted Chinchorro or Tarim mummies (the latter of which are natural and thus not really &quot;monster mummies&quot;, just dessicated corpses), nothing can really stand up to Iremite mummies in terms of how ancient they are. I mean, you can probably meet a five hundred years old vampire in WoD, and he doens't make you mad with just the accumulated weight of ages he carries, so either that's not enough or something much more specific about the Arisen causes Sybaris.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-30T10:25:11-07:00","id":903637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=903606#post903606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This, to me, is the big problem - why would any other mummy-like monster share this extremely specific and generally inefficient model of task-based existence? This level of &quot;evolutionary convergence&quot; would strike me as odd, especially seeing how the very idea of using the dead as lackeys is contrary to the reason any culture ever has produced mummies.</div> </div> </div> </div>And yet no one's questioning Egyptian ones being used as such. But I've remarked several times that some instances of mummification, including in the Andes, have partaken of messaging between the gods and the earth, or of ancestral reverence and concommittant belief that they provide some level of protection and guidance. A version of that with <i>actual</i> supernatural forces <i>does</i> lend itself to mummy alikes that solve specific problems for their people; you can't get away from that.<br /> <br /> Also, I answered the part about Descent in the post you quoted. The cyclic existence isn't even optimal for the Shan'iatu who instigated it - therefore, it may simply be a hard metaphisical limit that everyone has to put up with. Something you simply can't avoid when creating a being along such lines. In any case, a supernatural of similar lifestyle that led a life not using Descent-like mechanics would carry the implication that they were made with something better than the Rite of Return. While that wouldn't be a problem in a world by themselves (which actually is how I would probably use them), if they were sharing the world with the Deathless, I wouldn't want to make that implication - I would want to imply something lesser, but got right rather than got wrong.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'd contest that, considering how much older the Arisen are from anything else that could be made into a mummy-like undead. </div> </div> </div>I wasn't speaking in terms of mythos; I meant that any supernaturalised mummy is likely to have the power of the past over the present as a theme; and if you want to give that metaphysical heft, Sybaris is right there, and not especially Egyptian in concept. Disquiet might work as well, angry mobs have been a problem for mummies in film.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-30T11:43:12-07:00","id":903684,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=903637#post903637\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And yet no one's questioning Egyptian ones being used as such.</div> </div> </div> </div>I guess that's the reason for Arisen not actually being Egyptian.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But I've remarked several times that some instances of mummification, including in the Andes, have partaken of messaging between the gods and the earth, or of ancestral reverence and concommittant belief that they provide some level of protection and guidance. A version of that with <i>actual</i> supernatural forces <i>does</i> lend itself to mummy alikes that solve specific problems for their people; you can't get away from that. </div> </div> </div>As a side note: Suddenly I'm thinking maybe less people would have been sacrificed to the gods throughout the ages had said gods actually used the carcasses of those sacrifices to bring messages back to the people. Or maybe the opposite, hard to tell...<br /> <br /> That said, for me this similarity of function is exactly why I'm opposed to them having similar forms as Arisen as well, because that'd make them largely redundant and thus not that attractive as an addition to the game. Having a creature that performs a similar function but operates on a completely different paradigm is IMHO more interesting in terms of story potential.<br /> <br /> With that in mind, I can't shake the feeling that this story of children being sacrificed to the gods and coming back as magical envoys would work brilliantly as a creepy Sin-Eater Conspiracy of pre-Columbian Andes. It's also quite similar to the backstory of Ulgan Prometheans, now that I think about it. And if I were to run the &quot;Arisen meet Andean Mummies&quot; scenario, I'd rather take either of these than something too mechanically similar to the Arisen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, I answered the part about Descent in the post you quoted. The cyclic existence isn't even optimal for the Shan'iatu who instigated it - therefore, it may simply be a hard metaphisical limit that everyone has to put up with. Something you simply can't avoid when creating a being along such lines. In any case, a supernatural of similar lifestyle that led a life not using Descent-like mechanics would carry the implication that they were made with something better than the Rite of Return. While that wouldn't be a problem in a world by themselves (which actually is how I would probably use them), if they were sharing the world with the Deathless, I wouldn't want to make that implication - I would want to imply something lesser, but got right rather than got wrong. </div> </div> </div>Here's the rub - metaphysical hard limit on <i>what exactly?</i> There's at least ten different ways in WoD to raise a corpse as an intelligent undead, and each produces a completely different type of being with different powers and limitations. The Arisen are but one of them, and the only one to deal with this weird &quot;immortality in death&quot; deal. So it's not a hard limit on creating intelligent undead.<br /> <br /> What, then, is this common group on which this hard limit is imposed?<br /> <br /> Mummies?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I wasn't speaking in terms of mythos; I meant that any supernaturalised mummy is likely to have the power of the past over the present as a theme; and if you want to give that metaphysical heft, Sybaris is right there, and not especially Egyptian in concept. Disquiet might work as well, angry mobs have been a problem for mummies in film. </div> </div> </div>Again, I'd rather not have non-Arisen mummies with very similar themes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T08:45:04-07:00","id":904193,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=903684#post903684\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess that's the reason for Arisen not actually being Egyptian.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're Egyptian enough for you to use it as a rhetroical tool, so the least you can do is put up with me doing so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> With that in mind, I can't shake the feeling that this story of children being sacrificed to the gods and coming back as magical envoys would work brilliantly as a creepy Sin-Eater Conspiracy of pre-Columbian Andes. It's also quite similar to the backstory of Ulgan Prometheans, now that I think about it. </div> </div> </div>I have considered using Sineaters for such a thing, but it would need as much work to get to what I'm after as starting from the Deathless.<br /> <br /> [quote[What, then, is this common group on which this hard limit is imposed?[/quote]I've already answered that more than once, but remember my talking about the Messengers between heaven and earth angle? That. Beings that are part of both this world and a semi-divine otherworld. It makes sense as a hard limit that sometimes they have to go back there, if that's where their energy comes from. And doing otherwise would suggest they were made by something better than the Rite of Return.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mummies? </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I'm getting irritated at the implications that I could only want to use Mummy's mechanics because of a mummfied corpse being in the mix. You can't have ready my posts and honestly think that's where I'm coming from.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, I'd rather not have non-Arisen mummies with very similar themes. </div> </div> </div>I disagree. From the perspective of including such things in a Wicked Dead like supplement, which is the main subject of the thread, each sort of mummy-alike ought to throw a comparative focus on one or two aspects of the Deathless experience.<ul><li>In the case of Andean mummies, the best option would be ties to their people and messaging to heaven (cults and Descent/tasks) - where cults are not tools, but a focus of meaning and identity, making tasks (maybe) a source of joy - should the Deathless try to emulate that, or are the Mallqui duped and deluded?</li> <li>If I adapted a hardcore Buddhist monk mummy using Mummy mechanics (note I've already said I would use something else outside of a WD-alike context, see one paragraph back about irritation), then the most comparable aspect would probably be apotheosis (by implication Memory) and miraculous powers (seriously, lamas were credited with some epic shit) - can they provide insight that would serve the Deathless in a recovery of themselves, or given the self-abnegating aspects of Buddhism is it a dead end - or a sign that apotheosis itself is a dead end?</li> <li>In the case of fire-and-forget burials that happen to mummify (say the Paziryk ice mummies), it would be the body issues - the Sahu is a construct built around a corpse, which <i>probably</i> looks like the mummy did in life, but does it - or can it change, and if it can be changed, can it be changed willfully?</li> <li>In the case of fire-and-remember burials that are mummified deliberately, but got up accidentally (later Egyptian mummies, and Andean mummies could do for this too), I'd probably go with them loving their stuff, and throw a focus on the gathering of relics, and questions of who has the right to take them from whom.</li> <li>While they aren't mummified, voodoo zombies are, mythologically, slaves whose souls are captured, are made to menial, repetetive, and/or dangerous tasks, and who may be able to escape if they remember themselves - something that would resonate with the task-based and amnesic lives of the Deathless.</li> </ul><br /> And from the perspective of homebrewing, Mummy is the game with the tools to represent <i>all</i> these concepts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":321,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","user":"SunlessNick","date":". 05-31-2016, 11:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T09:38:57-07:00","id":904222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904193#post904193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>In the case of Andean mummies, the best option would be ties to their people and messaging to heaven (cults and Descent/tasks) - where cults are not tools, but a focus of meaning and identity, making tasks (maybe) a source of joy - should the Deathless try to emulate that, or are the Mallqui duped and deluded?</li> </ul><br /> </div> </div> </div> </div>A (Ghost) Revenant cult can't manage the same goal and message?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904193#post904193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>If I adapted a hardcore Buddhist monk mummy using Mummy mechanics (note I've already said I would use something else outside of a WD-alike context, see one paragraph back about irritation), then the most comparable aspect would probably be apotheosis (by implication Memory) and miraculous powers (seriously, lamas were credited with some epic shit) - can they provide insight that would serve the Deathless in a recovery of themselves, or given the self-abnegating aspects of Buddhism is it a dead end - or a sign that apotheosis itself is a dead end?</li> </ul><br /> </div> </div> </div> </div>Why are Mummy mechanics a better fit than Mage?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904193#post904193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>In the case of fire-and-forget burials that happen to mummify (say the Paziryk ice mummies), it would be the body issues - the Sahu is a construct built around a corpse, which <i>probably</i> looks like the mummy did in life, but does it - or can it change, and if it can be changed, can it be changed willfully?</li> </ul><br /> </div> </div> </div> </div>(Ghost) Revenant or Ulgan equally seems to fit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904193#post904193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>In the case of fire-and-remember burials that are mummified deliberately, but got up accidentally (later Egyptian mummies, and Andean mummies could do for this too), I'd probably go with them loving their stuff, and throw a focus on the gathering of relics, and questions of who has the right to take them from whom.</li> </ul><br /> </div> </div> </div> </div>(Ghost) Revenant seeking its Anchors.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904193#post904193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li>While they aren't mummified, voodoo zombies are, mythologically, slaves whose souls are captured, are made to menial, repetetive, and/or dangerous tasks, and who may be able to escape if they remember themselves - something that would resonate with the task-based and amnesic lives of the Deathless.</li> </ul><br /> <br /> And from the perspective of homebrewing, Mummy is the game with the tools to represent <i>all</i> these concepts.</div> </div> </div> </div>(Ghost) Revenant, Promethean, or amnesiac Sin-Eater.<br /> <br /> My point with these is - I can see already existing ways to fill these concepts. I'm not seeing how Mummy's metaphysics or mechanics actually serve as a better fit, and I've recast Mummy's mechanics into a Wuxia, Demon, and alt-Werewolf game (with partial work on an alt-Mage game).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T10:29:03-07:00","id":904255,"title":"","content":"Ok, you do realise that list was directly following on from a perspective about what could be in a Wicked Dead style supplement for Mummy, right? Why would you be proposing the use of Mage, Geist, or Promethean mechanics?<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T10:55:56-07:00","id":904280,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904255#post904255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ok, you do realise that list was directly following on from a perspective about what could be in a Wicked Dead style supplement for Mummy, right? Why would you be proposing the use of Mage, Geist, or Promethean mechanics?</div> </div> </div> </div>A Wicked Dead style supplement doesn't really make sense for Mummy. As has been pointed out, the metaphysics and mechanics for Mummy tightly bind them to their origin. Vampire's origin is purposefully murky, which support Wicked Dead's style.<br /> <br /> In all but one, a ghost-Revenant is a valid, Mummy compatible alternative. Or scaling that up into some kind of Lifeless, maybe. All of them are basically just asking for returned undead who share some Mummy themes.<br /> <br /> Mummies are what they are due to the Rite of Return. Even the Shuankhsen are a direct result of it. Everything flows from there. It would be like have Werewolves that don't come from Urfarah and Luna (in whatever confusing form/combination). It doesn't make any sense in the established metaphysics.<br /> <br /> Please explain in detail how each of these concepts <i>requires</i> Mummy mechanics? How are they better served that way than other existing options? Themes don't require matching mechanics.<br /> <br /> This isn't an attempt at shutting down the conversation - I'm just saying you need to be clearer with the design requirements. I can see how, in setting, you <i>could</i> have parallel development, or derivative development, but that doesn't seem to be what you want.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T11:41:02-07:00","id":904322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Pillars stem from an Egyptian view of the soul, or at least a bastardization of it, and as such make no sense for non-Iremite/Egyptian mummies. </div> </div> </div>Nitpic: A <i>Pre-</i>Egyptian view, and as beings of soul-stuff themselves, the argument can be made that the Shan'iatu had the right of it in their studies of the soul, and that later civilizations had a more...muddled understanding of the soul's core elements of Heart, Spirit, Essense, Name, and Shadow (Feeling, Desire to Act, Essential Being, (Self) Awareness, and Base Nature), which can still be found in other beings, if not so explicitly named.<br /> <br /> And once the Pillar issue is resolved, Decree can fall into place as well. While 'foreign' mummies will not have a Decree as such, most will still have a core sense of self linked to one of the five Pillars, like the Remnant of the Shuankhsen. Depending on the nature of the mummy's\u2019s particular form of return, however, some other factor may fill in this position, such as the Temakh of the Deceived.<br /> <br /> As for Memory, the individual 'sins' may need some tweaking, but for eternal beings that spend most of their time dead, a mechanic for recalling what it's like to be <b>alive</b>, and not becoming slaves to their 'programming', can work across the board.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T11:41:21-07:00","id":904323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904280#post904280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Wicked Dead style supplement doesn't really make sense for Mummy.</div> </div> </div> </div>How to include non-Iremite mummies has been one of the most prevalent subjects ever since the forum appeared (admiittedly so is Prometheans just shedding their weaknesses, rather than becoming human, which is an argument I've tended to be on the other side of - but I've at least made some attempt to engage with it). To my mind, the only way to include them would be a Wicked Dead style supplement. Hence I talk in those terms. I know it's hypothetical, and unlikely to ever occur, but that's not relevant factor in a thread expressly for the purpose of talking about non-Iremite mummies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mummies are what they are due to the Rite of Return. Even the Shuankhsen are a direct result of it. Everything flows from there. It would be like have Werewolves that don't come from Urfarah and Luna (in whatever confusing form/combination). It doesn't make any sense in the established metaphysics. </div> </div> </div>And Wicked Dead has vampires that are too far removed to come from the same originating force as the clans (and some that might not be). That's the point of that sort of supplement. And why it's the only sort of supplement in which non-Iremite mummies would have any place.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Please explain in detail how each of these concepts <i>requires</i> Mummy mechanics? How are they better served that way than other existing options? Themes don't require matching mechanics. </div> </div> </div>From the perspective of a hypothetical Wicked Dead-style supplement for Mummy, for the same reason Wicked Dead uses them for vampires that can't possibly be related to the Kindred. 1, Simplicity, because those mechanics are right there, and require fewer books on hand. 2, Robustness, because the mummy mechanics are more so than the ghost mechanics in the back (especially if you want to allow for them being playable). 3, Consistency, because even with alterations like the ones that exist in Wicked Dead, it makes it easier to compare the various monsters to Kindred and to one another. 4, To make it easier if you want the aforementioned &quot;can't possibly&quot; to turn out to be wroing, or maybe wrong.<br /> <br /> From the perspective of homebrewing for my own use, no I don't need to explain why a concept requires mummy mechanics for any reason except my preferring them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This isn't an attempt at shutting down the conversation </div> </div> </div>Well, you're the first poster in a while who's come across as actually engaging with what I'm saying instead of dismissing it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I'm just saying you need to be clearer with the design requirements. I can see how, in setting, you <i>could</i> have parallel development, or derivative development, but that doesn't seem to be what you want. </div> </div> </div>Ah, I think this might be part of the disconnect. I'm not exclusively talking about what I <i>want</i> I'm talking about how non-Iremite mummies <i>could</i> be included in a Mummy supplement without violating the singularity of the Shan'iatu's achievement.<br /> <br /> For the record, if I could have &quot;anything I wanted&quot; to represent the concepts in my last-but-one post's list, it would be, respectively. A game about ancestral protectors - a supernatural who's tied to a community/lineage/similar (most creatures need humans, but haven't had any for whom metaphysical ties to human populations have been the core of their power) - focussed on Andean Mallqui, but usuable for other variations on that theme (like say, adapting the concept of the Roman Genius). Purified being a full-on major template. Geist's 2nd Edition to be broadened to playing ghosts and revenants alongside Sineaters, with the Revenants having a Merit or condition where they're mummified. Same again, because Geist is pretty good for loving your stuff. And a chapter about being a zombie (living or dead) in a blue book supplement about broken states of mind, body, and spirit.<br /> <br /> But that's too much flight of fancy to dwell on here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-31T12:26:25-07:00","id":904357,"title":"","content":"Well, to deal with the essential premise inherent in the title of this thread, I can see 2 interpretations: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>The Mummies cannot be in any way derived or linked directly to Irem.</li> <li>The Mummies cannot originate from Irem (such as the Rite of Return).</li> </ol><br /> 2 allows for other successor cultures to create their own versions (bastardization optional) of the Rite of Return, which might be best for alt-Mummy models which deviate minimally from the Arisen model. 1 requires the most changes, since the Arisen template is <i>very</i> bound to its origins in Irem and the Rite of Return, though some parallel development also using Sekhem is possible (since Sekhem is supposed to just exist). Considering the intent is for a Wicked Dead style Supplement to Mummy, including at least a few 2 is desirable (since Mummy is very much focused on the legacy of Irem). 1s would be the curve-balls that shake things up. They <i>really</i> need an indepth look at what Sekhem <i>is</i>, though, to fully explore the potential ramifications.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904323#post904323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A game about ancestral protectors - a supernatural who's tied to a community/lineage/similar (most creatures need humans, but haven't had any for whom metaphysical ties to human populations have been the core of their power) - focussed on Andean Mallqui, but usuable for other variations on that theme (like say, adapting the concept of the Roman Genius).</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe tie the source(?) of their power/Sekhem to the community, such that the community/lineages' aggregate Sekhem/lifeforce is what is used to summon/fuel a revered ancestor/protector towards accomplishing some task. If Derived, since these hypothetical people don't want to chain <i>their</i> Mummies to the distant Judges, they need another source for their power - the Community at large. We know that there is some correlation or equivalency to mortal souls and Sekhem (there is a Relic that can convert a person's soul into a Sekhem dot), so perhaps instead of in one burst, a mass ritual draws forth a portion from a far larger number. This is done to accomplish a specific task that the community could not do otherwise. Ghost!Revenant might also have been incorporated into the design - the &quot;mummy&quot; is actually the person's ghost that is called back from the Underworld into their specifically prepared corpse, and &quot;Relics&quot; might be ritual Anchors used to keep them here (and additional Sekhem capacitors). The Sekhem is used to supercharge them from a &quot;mere&quot; ghost-possessed corpse into a mummy and grant them the power of Affinities (likely custom, since I doubt keeping the Five-Fold Soul idea of Pillars has merit for other cultures).<br /> <u>Plot Idea</u> - After the completion of the task, the mummy is <b>supposed</b> to return the borrowed Sekhem to their people, but some rare few [become corrupted/want to remain], and do not do so. They hunt and consume their own people to extend their stay (treat eating their people as consuming a Relic).<br /> <u>Mechanics</u> - Keep most of a Mummy's inherent traits. Sekhem likely capped at less than 10 (5 or community dependent?). No Pillars likely. This means no Pillar points to fuel Utterances (which is fine, because these seem less tied to Fate and Cosmic Power). Might sub in Numina or Dread Powers if keeping the Ghost!Revenant base - Might possess an Essence pool in that case. Essence pool would also bypass the lack of Pillar points for Attribute buffing and healing - just let them use it like ephemerals do. Affinities don't require Pillars (merely Willpower, upon occasion), so a lack of Pillar points won't hold them back. Descent is justified as the Sekhem leaking from them. First Purpose is their given task. Memory could be justified as the typical memory/identity degradation ghosts go through. Cult is the community. Lacks a Tomb? One the one hand, the source culture doesn't have Iremite knowledge of Geomancy, but they might possess their own.<br /> <br /> <u>Random Mechanics Ideas</u>:<ul><li>Has only a single Pillar (restricting Mummy Affinities, Utterances, etc.)</li> <li>Tie Pillar equivalents to external things (ritualists or bloodline members, Relic/receptacles, etc.)</li> <li>Living Mummies? Mortals infused with the power of Sekhem? What would that do? Utterances?</li> <li>Greater focus on Geomancy - uses it to support the &quot;mummy&quot; and their abilities?</li> </ul><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 05-31-2016, 03:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T03:47:20-07:00","id":904835,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904193#post904193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They're Egyptian enough for you to use it as a rhetroical tool, so the least you can do is put up with me doing so.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're also not-Egyptian enough to dodge accusations of cultural insensitivity (I'd go so far as to assume this was a specific consideration when MtC was first penned, as WW/OPP have gone a long way in this regard from the days of oWoD Mummy, KotE and Gypsies), so I'm lukewarm on this particular use of said rhetorical tool.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have considered using Sineaters for such a thing, but it would need as much work to get to what I'm after as starting from the Deathless. </div> </div> </div>For what I'm beginning to understand you're after, apparently. For what I'd be after were I to run &quot;Arisen meet Andean Ice Mummies&quot;, either the Sin-Eaters or the Ulgan would work pretty much as is. Or, hell, even the corpse-Claimed or Revenants.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I've already answered that more than once, but remember my talking about the Messengers between heaven and earth angle? That. Beings that are part of both this world and a semi-divine otherworld. It makes sense as a hard limit that sometimes they have to go back there, if that's where their energy comes from. And doing otherwise would suggest they were made by something better than the Rite of Return. </div> </div> </div>Except... Ulgan Prometheans are also envoys between people and spirits (or at least they started that way), and they work completely differently from the Arisen. Sin-Eaters are (among other things) envoys between the living and the dead, and they work differently from both Arisen and Ulgan. The messenger angle doesn't demand mechanical similarity with the Arisen, and frankly, it's too common a theme for various grossly different beings in WoD to use it to delineate any common subset of creatures.<br /> <br /> The Rite of Return was the best act of magic possible... for creating Arisen. Any attempt to create more Arisen should be less successful than the Rite to preserve the themes of the game. But it's by no means some universal benchmark for other supernatural beings, or other undead beings in particular, or any arbitrary category of beings you'd care to name. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yeah, I'm getting irritated at the implications that I could only want to use Mummy's mechanics because of a mummfied corpse being in the mix. You can't have ready my posts and honestly think that's where I'm coming from. </div> </div> </div>Sorry, but where I'm coming from in this thread boils down to &quot;why would you do that at all?!&quot;, so I'm iffy on your side of argument's motivations at best. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I disagree. From the perspective of including such things in a Wicked Dead like supplement, which is the main subject of the thread, each sort of mummy-alike ought to throw a comparative focus on one or two aspects of the Deathless experience. </div> </div> </div>Wicked Dead again... to add to already listed arguments as to why the WD format doesn't work for MtC: in Vampire, being suddenly plunged into a secret world that makes no sense is an important theme, and both the unexplained source of vampirism and various bizarre permutations presented in WD serve to reinforce it.<br /> <br /> This is almost perfectly opposite to the themes of Mummy, where being an eldritch abomination doing the chores for Lovecraftian gods as part of an ancient conspiracy is supposed to be the familiar part of your existence, contrasted with the ever-changing mundane world that's supposed to keep giving you pause with its vicissitudes throughout the ages. The WD format can only muddle the themes of MtC, not expand or improve them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <ul><li>In the case of Andean mummies, the best option would be ties to their people and messaging to heaven (cults and Descent/tasks) - where cults are not tools, but a focus of meaning and identity, making tasks (maybe) a source of joy - should the Deathless try to emulate that, or are the Mallqui duped and deluded?</li> <li>If I adapted a hardcore Buddhist monk mummy using Mummy mechanics (note I've already said I would use something else outside of a WD-alike context, see one paragraph back about irritation), then the most comparable aspect would probably be apotheosis (by implication Memory) and miraculous powers (seriously, lamas were credited with some epic shit) - can they provide insight that would serve the Deathless in a recovery of themselves, or given the self-abnegating aspects of Buddhism is it a dead end - or a sign that apotheosis itself is a dead end?</li> <li>In the case of fire-and-forget burials that happen to mummify (say the Paziryk ice mummies), it would be the body issues - the Sahu is a construct built around a corpse, which <i>probably</i> looks like the mummy did in life, but does it - or can it change, and if it can be changed, can it be changed willfully?</li> <li>In the case of fire-and-remember burials that are mummified deliberately, but got up accidentally (later Egyptian mummies, and Andean mummies could do for this too), I'd probably go with them loving their stuff, and throw a focus on the gathering of relics, and questions of who has the right to take them from whom.</li> <li>While they aren't mummified, voodoo zombies are, mythologically, slaves whose souls are captured, are made to menial, repetetive, and/or dangerous tasks, and who may be able to escape if they remember themselves - something that would resonate with the task-based and amnesic lives of the Deathless.</li> </ul><br /> And from the perspective of homebrewing, Mummy is the game with the tools to represent <i>all</i> these concepts. </div> </div> </div>The way I see it, since these themes are by definition already handled by the Arisen and related monsters, introducing a new being just to present a slight permutation on said themes is akin to starting your own pizzeria because the crust to cheese ratio in the existing ones is not quite exactly to your liking.<br /> <br /> No, if you want to really add onto those themes, you need something that approaches the same themes in a drastically different manner.<br /> <br /> If you want to explore the theme of duty, either to gods or to cults, don't introduce a being that works almost exactly like the Arisen but likes it, because some Arisen already like it - loyalists are a thing, and so are mummies who actually care about their cults and devote every spare moment of their Descents to helping them. Rather, introduce a being that isn't beholden to their gods and/or their cults in the way Arisen are, yet performs similar functions out of its own free will, because that offers a completely different perspective to the Arisen who don't really have a choice in the matter.<br /> <br /> If you want to raise questions about Apotheosis, don't introduce beings that are Ascended Arisen in all but name, because we've already got Apotheosis and mummies who seek or attained it. Rather, introduce a being that considers the default state of Arisen superior to his brand of immortality. Maybe the past deeds burden him, and he envies the Arisen their lack of memory. Maybe he's damn tired but cannot die, and envies the Arisen their long bouts of peace in the afterlife. Maybe he's seen enough of his plans and hopes turn to dust that the perspective of waking up in a completely new and unknown world excites him.<br /> <br /> So on and so forth. The themes of MtC are sufficiently handled by existing MtC options (it'd be a badly designed game if they weren't). To expand them, to put a new spin on them, you need a highly contrasting perspective, not more of the same with slight alterations.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T04:44:13-07:00","id":904855,"title":"","content":"To be honest, this whole thread sort of highlights why I think Mummy doesn't make any sense as a Chronicles of Darkness gameline... Not anywhere near enough adaptability if so many people get so annoyed with the idea of a mummy with backstory that isn't from a specific region, specific social class, and specific era.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T05:14:40-07:00","id":904869,"title":"","content":"That's actually a fair point, regardless of my opinion on the inclusion of non-Iremite Mummies. Other game-lines are similar, though, as a counter-point: <b>Demon</b>, for one, because of distaste towards the 'tech-gnostic' part of it; this is also actually the reason I don't like <b>Changeling</b>, too. Sure you can have kiths from all over the world but it stretches my suspension of disbelief to say all these fae running around were kidnapped and then escaped.<br /> <br /> So...fair point, but one that you could argue for probably any of the games if you squint enough","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T05:35:54-07:00","id":904878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>reseru</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904869#post904869\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's actually a fair point, regardless of my opinion on the inclusion of non-Iremite Mummies. Other game-lines are similar, though, as a counter-point: <b>Demon</b>, for one, because of distaste towards the 'tech-gnostic' part of it; this is also actually the reason I don't like <b>Changeling</b>, too. Sure you can have kiths from all over the world but it stretches my suspension of disbelief to say all these fae running around were kidnapped and then escaped.<br /> <br /> So...fair point, but one that you could argue for probably any of the games if you squint enough</div> </div> </div> </div>1. It's easy to ignore the tech-gnostic-ness of demon, non-robot demons and non-robot angels are a thing in the game without any homebrewing.<br /> 2. Kidnapping isn't actually required in Changeling, you can go there willingly.<br /> 3. Characters of a template having a single common element to their backstory, such as &quot;Escaped arcadia&quot;, is nowhere near 90% of your backstory is the same for every character, where every single one has to be from the same country/time/origin/social caste.<br /> 4. Changelings can not only be from all over the world, but from any time period, can be from any social caste, can have entered arcadia for a variety of different reasons, can have had anything imaginable occur during your durance, and can do anything once you've left your durance.<br /> <br /> All gamelines have common elements in the origins of their template, but Mummy goes &quot;YOU HAVE ONE BACKSTORY TO CHOOSE FROM&quot;. It's possible to gain a bit of backstory, like snippets of your life (in one specific region/time/social caste that you shared with every single other mummy) or few short-duration times you were previously resurrected.... once you spend enough experience on memory.... So yeah.... if you think that applies to all gamelines if you squint enough, I think you have your eyes closed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 06-01-2016, 08:41 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T06:00:04-07:00","id":904893,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904878#post904878\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. It's easy to ignore the tech-gnostic-ness of demon, non-robot demons and non-robot angels are a thing in the game without any homebrewing.<br /> 2. Kidnapping isn't actually required in Changeling, you can go there willingly.<br /> 3. Characters of a template having a single common element to their backstory, such as &quot;Escaped arcadia&quot;, is nowhere near 90% of your backstory is the same for every character, where every single one has to be from the same country/time/origin/social caste.<br /> 4. Changelings can not only be from all over the world, but from any time period, can be from any social caste, can have entered arcadia for a variety of different reasons, can have had anything imaginable occur during your durance, and can do anything once you've left your durance.<br /> <br /> All gamelines have common elements in the origins of their template, but Mummy goes &quot;YOU HAVE ONE BACKSTORY TO CHOOSE FROM&quot;. It's possible to gain a bit of backstory, like snippets of your life (in one specific region/time/social caste that you shared with every single other mummy) or few short-duration times you were previously resurrected.... once you spend enough experience on memory.... So yeah.... if you think that applies to all gamelines if you squint enough, I think you have your eyes closed.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I disagree with your end conclusion, everything leading up to it is right and correct.<br /> <br /> But I think saying Mummy only gives you one backstory minimizes and ignores the width and breadth that the Nameless offers. A slave who beat out a bar of bronze and attached it to the Mesen-Nebu's gate is a very different story from a Scribe descended from a family of Scribes, or how two servants of the Keeper did very different things.<br /> <br /> Yes, y'all are from the same Empire. That's one factor in a sea of thousands.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T06:01:09-07:00","id":904894,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904835#post904835\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry, but where I'm coming from in this thread boils down to &quot;why would you do that at all?!&quot;, so I'm iffy on your side of argument's motivations at best.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well if you won't engage with me even enough to accept my motivations for what they are, why should I care about anything you say? I think we're done.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904357#post904357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Maybe tie the source(?) of their power/Sekhem to the community, such that the community/lineages' aggregate Sekhem/lifeforce is what is used to summon/fuel a revered ancestor/protector towards accomplishing some task. If Derived, since these hypothetical people don't want to chain <i>their</i> Mummies to the distant Judges, they need another source for their power - the Community at large. We know that there is some correlation or equivalency to mortal souls and Sekhem (there is a Relic that can convert a person's soul into a Sekhem dot), so perhaps instead of in one burst, a mass ritual draws forth a portion from a far larger number. This is done to accomplish a specific task that the community could not do otherwise. Ghost!Revenant might also have been incorporated into the design - the &quot;mummy&quot; is actually the person's ghost that is called back from the Underworld into their specifically prepared corpse, and &quot;Relics&quot; might be ritual Anchors used to keep them here (and additional Sekhem capacitors).</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the kind of thing I'd been thinking of, yes. The size of their community and strength of their ties to it would determine their power stat. (This has a knock on effect of making this a &quot;seek temporal power&quot; splat, which is something ChroD has backed off from so far, even in the case of Vampire) (it also makes it a supernatural for which ties to humanity are its metaphysical basis). Rather than the Underworld, they could spend most of their time with their spirits diffused into those of their people - called back when summoned, or when this communion alerts them to a danger the individuals don't see.<br /> <br /> Relics probably wouldn't be a factor, though, or a major part of their lifestyle.<br /> <br /> Something else I'd considered was that equivalent - let's say Ancestors as a general term - elsewhere in the world might not use their mummified bodies. Some might possess a high priest figure (cf the way the Roman Genius was said to inhabit the head of the family); some might manifest as ghosts or voices; others might even still be alive (in which case, the aforementioned communion wouldn't be their default state, but one they have to deliberately use - which means they would lack a lot of the insights into their people others are capable of).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Sekhem is used to supercharge them from a &quot;mere&quot; ghost-possessed corpse into a mummy and grant them the power of Affinities (likely custom, since I doubt keeping the Five-Fold Soul idea of Pillars has merit for other cultures). </div> </div> </div>Custom, for sure (though some of the canon ones would adapt easily enough).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <u>Plot Idea</u> - After the completion of the task, the mummy is <b>supposed</b> to return the borrowed Sekhem to their people, but some rare few [become corrupted/want to remain], and do not do so. They hunt and consume their own people to extend their stay (treat eating their people as consuming a Relic). </div> </div> </div>Indeed. Their power coming partly from their ties to their people (and how many there are) provides an incentive not to do that, but people wanting to stay up and about walking the Earth don't always make the best long term decisions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <u>Mechanics</u> - Keep most of a Mummy's inherent traits. Sekhem likely capped at less than 10 (5 or community dependent?). No Pillars likely. This means no Pillar points to fuel Utterances (which is fine, because these seem less tied to Fate and Cosmic Power). Might sub in Numina or Dread Powers if keeping the Ghost!Revenant base - Might possess an Essence pool in that case. Essence pool would also bypass the lack of Pillar points for Attribute buffing and healing - just let them use it like ephemerals do. Affinities don't require Pillars (merely Willpower, upon occasion), so a lack of Pillar points won't hold them back. Descent is justified as the Sekhem leaking from them. First Purpose is their given task. Memory could be justified as the typical memory/identity degradation ghosts go through. Cult is the community. Lacks a Tomb? One the one hand, the source culture doesn't have Iremite knowledge of Geomancy, but they might possess their own. </div> </div> </div>Sekhem, or whatever I call it, would be capped at the power stat determined by their communities. No Pillars, but probably a fuel stat (not Essence, though - living Ancestors would have a much harder time replenishing it). I'd probably stick with adapted and custom Affinities - no Utterances (too powerful as well as anything else). Descent would be a leak of power from forming a single incarnation (mallqui would lost power faster, those inhabiting a human body would lost it more slowly, living ones might not lose it at all). I wouldn't use a Tomb for these (partly to allow options with a &quot;focal manifestation&quot; other than a mummy, and also because mallqui didn't always have tombs in the sense that the Tomb Merit implies.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Tie Pillar equivalents to external things (ritualists or bloodline members, Relic/receptacles, etc.) </div> </div> </div>This isn't something I'd do with mallqui/ancestors (no Pillars). But, with relics, it would be an interesting option for any later Egyptian mummies who start walking around. (And would add an additional moral dimension for Deathless who want to feed those relics to Duat)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Living Mummies? Mortals infused with the power of Sekhem? What would that do? Utterances? </div> </div> </div>I mentioned living ancestors to go with the mallqui. When I mentioned voodoo zombies, I also wasn't thinking in terms of them necessarily being dead.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Greater focus on Geomancy - uses it to support the &quot;mummy&quot; and their abilities? </div> </div> </div>Well, Andean civilisations were capable of some <i>amazing</i> earthworks. There's one valley that, at first, seemed to be a model of a particular astrological conjunction, but which on closer examination turned out to be a very precise near miss of a conjunction that was considered to bring misfortune - and led to a theory that it was an attempt to &quot;hack&quot; the sky into missing that particualar conjunction out.<br /> <br /> Thanks, anyway, that was useful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T06:39:57-07:00","id":904919,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904855#post904855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be honest, this whole thread sort of highlights why I think Mummy doesn't make any sense as a Chronicles of Darkness gameline... Not anywhere near enough adaptability if so many people get so annoyed with the idea of a mummy with backstory that isn't from a specific region, specific social class, and specific era.</div> </div> </div> </div>CofD games are built on limitation, restriction and separation. A vampire is this and this, but not that. A werewolf can do X and Y, but not Z. This is why we're buying distinct gamelines instead of playing freeform monsters built with dread powers or whatnot - we thrive on these restrictions!<br /> <br /> MtC isn't any more restrictive than any other CofD game, but it draws its lines in different places than the others to achieve a different feel and explore different themes - but it does so brilliantly, and the breadth and depth of stories you can tell with it is no less than any other major CofD title.<br /> <br /> Also, I think you're being a bit disingenuous with how you present the restrictions as ultra-specific, where actually your ethnicity can be any from the Middle East or North Africa and your original social class ranged from trusted laborer foremen to pampered artisans to the clergy, administrators and lawmen of Irem, i.e. roughly anyone in the Empire who wasn't either a Shan'iatu, a Pharaoh, or a menial worker.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T07:21:27-07:00","id":904944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904894#post904894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is the kind of thing I'd been thinking of, yes. The size of their community and strength of their ties to it would determine their power stat. (This has a knock on effect of making this a &quot;seek temporal power&quot; splat, which is something ChroD has backed off from so far, even in the case of Vampire) (it also makes it a supernatural for which ties to humanity are its metaphysical basis). Rather than the Underworld, they could spend most of their time with their spirits diffused into those of their people - called back when summoned, or when this communion alerts them to a danger the individuals don't see.</div> </div> </div> </div>I went with Ghost!Revenant because that was the most similar and accomplishes the most objectives without making something else new. I try to reuse as much as possible, both to expand existing examples/permutations, and to reduce work.<br /> <br /> Something that is &quot;sourced&quot; from the collective community itself might be drawn from the Astral. Injecting Sekhem into a Goetic?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904894#post904894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Relics probably wouldn't be a factor, though, or a major part of their lifestyle.</div> </div> </div> </div>&lt;shrug&gt; I was just rying to tie as much Mummy as possible in. The parallels to Anchors seemed fitting for this interpretation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904894#post904894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Something else I'd considered was that equivalent - let's say Ancestors as a general term - elsewhere in the world might not use their mummified bodies. Some might possess a high priest figure (cf the way the Roman Genius was said to inhabit the head of the family); some might manifest as ghosts or voices; others might even still be alive (in which case, the aforementioned communion wouldn't be their default state, but one they have to deliberately use - which means they would lack a lot of the insights into their people others are capable of).</div> </div> </div> </div>This sounds like either co-opting a family Spirit, or maybe that Goetic+Sekhem idea above. The initial creation Rite solidifies an identity, but parts are drawn from the family/community. The result is given body/power/cohesion/agency by the Sekhem.<br /> <br /> Not their mummified bodies (possibly because they never had one to begin with): Manifestation.<br /> Tied to existing person: Possession (or Fetter).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904894#post904894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Indeed. Their power coming partly from their ties to their people (and how many there are) provides an incentive not to do that, but people wanting to stay up and about walking the Earth don't always make the best long term decisions.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, greed doesn't always make long term sense. Technically, even if the whole community is killed off, the !mummy would remain (until the Sekhem bled out), but then there would be no way to re-raise them. Opps.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904894#post904894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sekhem, or whatever I call it, would be capped at the power stat determined by their communities. No Pillars, but probably a fuel stat (not Essence, though - living Ancestors would have a much harder time replenishing it).</div> </div> </div> </div>Sekhem is Sekhem no matter what it is called. It exists enough for LDI to extract and make medicine out of, so it exists. As for Essence, it's a nice common fuel stat. If you were basing living mummies off Purified, well, Purified use it too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>SunlessNick</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904894#post904894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This isn't something I'd do with mallqui/ancestors (no Pillars). But, with relics, it would be an interesting option for any later Egyptian mummies who start walking around. (And would add an additional moral dimension for Deathless who want to feed those relics to Duat)<br /> <br /> Well, Andean civilisations were capable of some <i>amazing</i> earthworks. There's one valley that, at first, seemed to be a model of a particular astrological conjunction, but which on closer examination turned out to be a very precise near miss of a conjunction that was considered to bring misfortune - and led to a theory that it was an attempt to &quot;hack&quot; the sky into missing that particualar conjunction out.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was just spitballing ideas for other variations. Arisen (or Deceived) encountering others with similar (or superior) uses of Geomancy would certainly shake them up.<br /> <br /> Of course your above example <i>totally</i> sounds like Infrastructure.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904919#post904919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, I think you're being a bit disingenuous with how you present the restrictions as ultra-specific, where actually your ethnicity can be any from the Middle East or North Africa and your original social class ranged from trusted laborer foremen to pampered artisans to the clergy, administrators and lawmen of Irem, i.e. roughly anyone in the Empire who wasn't either a Shan'iatu, a Pharaoh, or a menial worker.</div> </div> </div> </div>Technically, only not Shan'iatu (but that's because they are non-human entirely). You might be able to pull off a good story about the other two, such as a slave/laborer that was accidentally included in the Rite of Return, or a disgraced Pharaoh (who wasn't killed).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T15:59:11-07:00","id":905237,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904878#post904878\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> All gamelines have common elements in the origins of their template, but Mummy goes &quot;YOU HAVE ONE BACKSTORY TO CHOOSE FROM&quot;. It's possible to gain a bit of backstory, like snippets of your life (in one specific region/time/social caste that you shared with every single other mummy) or few short-duration times you were previously resurrected.... once you spend enough experience on memory.... So yeah.... if you think that applies to all gamelines if you squint enough, I think you have your eyes closed.</div> </div> </div> </div>This kind of comment makes me wonder if you think the only thing defining a person is their place in their society. Which is hilarious, because Mummy basically spends a good chunk of its mechanics creating the <i>opposite</i> impression.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T19:10:29-07:00","id":905326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904919#post904919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">CofD games are built on limitation, restriction and separation. A vampire is this and this, but not that. A werewolf can do X and Y, but not Z. This is why we're buying distinct gamelines instead of playing freeform monsters built with dread powers or whatnot - we thrive on these restrictions!</div> </div> </div> </div>I love how the line between freeform and not freeform is whether the game says you have to be from Ancient Irem. &quot;You can't be a mummy from the Andes or it's freeform!&quot; :P<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> MtC isn't any more restrictive than any other CofD game, but it draws its lines in different places than the others to achieve a different feel and explore different themes - but it does so brilliantly, and the breadth and depth of stories you can tell with it is no less than any other major CofD title. </div> </div> </div>It is much much much more restrictive.... The number of backstories you have is significantly less than any other gameline. That's not to say I don't think it does so with a purpose though. It restricts the flavour of characters so that it can highlight it's themes in the best way the writer can think of and to give a rich world to play in. And how many potential backstories a character can have has no real correlation to how deep or insightful the stories that the game can create are, so I'm not sure why your mentioning that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, I think you're being a bit disingenuous with how you present the restrictions as ultra-specific, where actually your ethnicity can be any from the Middle East or North Africa and your original social class ranged from trusted laborer foremen to pampered artisans to the clergy, administrators and lawmen of Irem, i.e. roughly anyone in the Empire who wasn't either a Shan'iatu, a Pharaoh, or a menial worker. </div> </div> </div>I disagree, since that still sounds pretty ultra-specific to me compared to any of the other gamelines, or really any other RPG I have played.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":8331,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","user":"milo v3","date":". 06-01-2016, 10:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T20:05:15-07:00","id":905350,"title":"","content":"Regarding: Having to originate from Irem<br /> <br /> Meh. So you all came from some ancient empire over a hundred year or so span. From multiple walks of life. So what? That is such a small part of any individual Mummy's <i>many </i><i>thousand years</i> of other backstory, and it only become relevant at the very highest points of Memory. I find it about as relevant as &quot;all Werewolves came from Urfarah and Luna&quot; or &quot;all Vampires died before the Embrace&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T20:14:52-07:00","id":905358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905326#post905326\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is much much much more restrictive.... The number of backstories you have is significantly less than any other gameline.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you define backstory solely by what your home country is, sure. Most people who don't have an axe to grind aren't going to be that needlessly reductive.<br /> <br /> As Vent highlights, in the time between that point of origin and the modern day you've likely crossed oceans, diverted history, and missed out on entire generations of human activity, all of which is directly relevant to the parts of the game that aren't the most immediate framework for a what-are-we-doing-and-why-are-we-here relic-retrieval setup.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T20:15:47-07:00","id":905360,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905350#post905350\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Regarding: Having to originate from Irem<br /> <br /> Meh. So you all came from some ancient empire over a hundred year or so span. From multiple walks of life. So what? That is such a small part of any individual Mummy's <i>many </i><i>thousand years</i> of other backstory, and it only become relevant at the very highest points of Memory. I find it about as relevant as &quot;all Werewolves came from Urfarah and Luna&quot; or &quot;all Vampires died before the Embrace&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Then people should stop trying to shut down non-Irem mummies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/904596-geniuses-and-gramarie\" target=\"_blank\">Genius templates</a>: Super Science Mini-Template for Demon: the Descent<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1337874-oracle-the-endbringers-fan-splat\" target=\"_blank\">Oracle the Endbringers</a>: Time-Manipulator Fan-Splat","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8331-milo-v3","name":"milo v3","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8331_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1505"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T20:46:33-07:00","id":905375,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905360#post905360\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then people should stop trying to shut down non-Irem mummies.</div> </div> </div> </div>Exactly <i>what</i> those non-Irem mummies are, though, and why they are still &quot;mummies&quot; is a bit complicated, as previously discussed.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T21:08:18-07:00","id":905389,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>milo v3</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905360#post905360\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then people should stop trying to shut down non-Irem mummies.</div> </div> </div> </div>Note that the only backstory the player actually gets to control is their most recent Descent. Not their time in Irem. Your objection that your backstory is limited to Irem is wrong because that isn't the backstory you create. Your backstory as a player of Mummy is of any time or place or social standing in the <i>entire scope of human history</i>. That's not limited. Saying so is just... I dunno. I got nothing. The word &quot;wrong&quot; is the only one that suits. I hesitate to toss around words like &quot;objective&quot;, but in this case I think I could genuinely get a mathematical proof to demonstrate it.<br /> <br /> Your ultimate origin is restricted, sure. Your ethnicity... kinda is, but only if you're not comfortable playing Twice-Arisen. But the former is simply in-world mythology, there's no need for it to inform who your character is unless you want to. Maybe your last 500 years of immersion in Central American culture is more important to your Mummy than descents so much more distant they may as well have happened to someone else? Does the fact that you were born by the Nile <i>matter</i> in any but the most pedantic fashion? Next to the breadth and variety that Mummy offers, is the fact that your character's look going to be informed by one of the dozen or so ethnic groups in the Middle East and North-Central Africa really important?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T21:25:16-07:00","id":905396,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=904919#post904919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">CofD games are built on limitation, restriction and separation. A vampire is this and this, but not that. A werewolf can do X and Y, but not Z. This is why we're buying distinct gamelines instead of playing freeform monsters built with dread powers or whatnot - we thrive on these restrictions!<br /> <br /> MtC isn't any more restrictive than any other CofD game, but it draws its lines in different places than the others to achieve a different feel and explore different themes - but it does so brilliantly, and the breadth and depth of stories you can tell with it is no less than any other major CofD title.<br /> <br /> Also, I think you're being a bit disingenuous with how you present the restrictions as ultra-specific, where actually your ethnicity can be any from the Middle East or North Africa and your original social class ranged from trusted laborer foremen to pampered artisans to the clergy, administrators and lawmen of Irem, i.e. roughly anyone in the Empire who wasn't either a Shan'iatu, a Pharaoh, or a menial worker.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you have some problematic assumptions here. One thing that strikes me is that CofD games are all sandbox games and that they have a means of varying their rules and settings called Twists.<br /> <br /> The second problem is that mummification was practiced by many cultures. North Africa hardly has a monopoly on the practice. Repeated arguments against is more than a little white-washy. It would be sad for OPP to develop a &quot;white&quot; problem like the MCU has.<br /> <br /> Third, Iremites must be the best secret keepers ever because the secret never gets out, but if you know anything about how technological innovation propagates across the world then it really doesn't make sense. No one's achievements are unique. Things get invented in multiple places all the time. Sometimes simultaneously.<br /> <br /> So yes, I'd expect a 2e storytellers guide to address this obvious gap. Mummy has an unique place in that it's the most obviously linked to a single specific extinct culture. However, that also makes it a game in the most precarious position of looking racist by priveleging one extinct culture over countless others. I'd hate my favorite 1e game to become the elephant in the room.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1630-jacob","name":"Jacob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1630_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"681"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T22:00:31-07:00","id":905406,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you have some problematic assumptions here. One thing that strikes me is that CofD games are all sandbox games and that they have a means of varying their rules and settings called Twists.<br /> <br /> The second problem is that mummification was practiced by many cultures. North Africa hardly has a monopoly on the practice. Repeated arguments against is more than a little white-washy. It would be sad for OPP to develop a &quot;white&quot; problem like the MCU has.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but the practice of mummification that Mummy is based on is 100% Egyptian. There's no cultural similarity between, to use the most popular two, Egyptian mummification and Incan mummification beyond the end result of a preserved corpse. They're entirely separate cultural practices, and saying that the two are in any way related because English uses the same word to describe them is a worse act of cultural generalisation than any &quot;whitewashing&quot; you think might be going on by saying &quot;Egyptian mummies are the one true mummies&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Third, Iremites must be the best secret keepers ever because the secret never gets out, but if you know anything about how technological innovation propagates across the world then it really doesn't make sense. No one's achievements are unique. Things get invented in multiple places all the time. Sometimes simultaneously.</div> </div> </div> </div>Last I checked, that's why relics exist in literally every culture. Those were Irem's technology innovation, after all. The reason why Mummies are unique is more because those who knew the ritual were inhuman avatars of Lovecraftian entities obsessed with self-grandisement at the cost of an entire civilisation of living souls. They didn't share that info on the Rite around too much. Even some of the Shan'iatu were kept in the dark about elements of it, as the Deceived can testify. And any living soul who witnessed it was either on the receiving end or consigned directly to Duat. Then there's the implication from Book of the Deceived that the world was inherently more magical in the time of Irem, that rituals on the scale of the Rite may not actually be possible in the modern magic climate. It's possible that a change to the fundamental laws of physics in the intervening time may render it impossible for a later civilisation to replicate a past one's technological achievements.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So yes, I'd expect a 2e storytellers guide to address this obvious gap. Mummy has an unique place in that it's the most obviously linked to a single specific extinct culture. However, that also makes it a game in the most precarious position of looking racist by priveleging one extinct culture over countless others. I'd hate my favorite 1e game to become the elephant in the room.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've tried to write a response to this several times now. I'm getting nothing. This is silly. No one's saying white people or South American people can't be Mummies due to any element of race.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T22:09:29-07:00","id":905410,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One thing that strikes me is that CofD games are all sandbox games and that they have a means of varying their rules and settings called Twists.</div> </div> </div> </div>The games are very much not built on the premise that the players and incidental NPCs are the only source of plot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The second problem is that mummification was practiced by many cultures. </div> </div> </div>And very few of those cultures have anything on par with the impact that Egyptomania and Hammer Horror provided to the pyramids-and-deserts variety of monsters-made-of-ritually-preserved corpses.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Third, Iremites must be the best secret keepers ever because the secret never gets out, but if you know anything about how technological innovation propagates across the world then it really doesn't make sense. No one's achievements are unique. Things get invented in multiple places all the time. Sometimes simultaneously. </div> </div> </div>The Rite of Return is not technology, is not an invention, required massive literal blood sacrifice to the cosmic force of entropy, broke/warped/reshaped the cosmos upon its completion, and moved the entities with the closest thing to a complete picture of its workings into the afterlife as part of its design.<br /> <br /> This is a spurious excuse for a &quot;problem.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-02T06:57:29-07:00","id":905594,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The second problem is that mummification was practiced by many cultures.</div> </div> </div> </div>But the Rite of Return wasn't.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Third, Iremites must be the best secret keepers ever because the secret never gets out, but if you know anything about how technological innovation propagates across the world then it really doesn't make sense. No one's achievements are unique. Things get invented in multiple places all the time. Sometimes simultaneously.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kinda difficult when the aforementioned Rite of Return <i>broke</i> Time, such that it's exact recreation is implied to be impossible. But by all means if other societies guided by god-monsters like the Shan'iatu want to try...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So yes, I'd expect a 2e storytellers guide to address this obvious gap. Mummy has an unique place in that it's the most obviously linked to a single specific extinct culture. However, that also makes it a game in the most precarious position of looking racist by priveleging one extinct culture over countless others. I'd hate my favorite 1e game to become the elephant in the room.</div> </div> </div> </div>Except, it isn't at all racist. Arisen, Shuankhsen, and Deceived all originate from the Rite of Return, which was, by nature, performed once. Irem built that Wonder of The World, no one else got to first. Facts of (Fictional) Life. Trying to water things down by saying one group's achievement was replicated by disparate other cultures, just because they happened to preserve their dead is... I don't know the word for it.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-02T07:00:32-07:00","id":905595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905358#post905358\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you define backstory solely by what your home country is, sure. Most people who don't have an axe to grind aren't going to be that needlessly reductive.<br /> <br /> As Vent highlights, in the time between that point of origin and the modern day you've likely crossed oceans, diverted history, and missed out on entire generations of human activity, all of which is directly relevant to the parts of the game that aren't the most immediate framework for a what-are-we-doing-and-why-are-we-here relic-retrieval setup.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is why the idea of a Wicked Dead style supplement keeps coming up. Sekhem is a force that, canonically, exists all over the world. The techniques of the Shan'iatu are, canonically, not the only things that can manipulate it (hell, <i>techniques</i> aren't the only things). Lesser versions of the Rite have canonically been attempted (though it's not canonical whether or not they've produced anything worthwhile). Sothic turns, such as when the game is set by default, are when the Deathless frequently go wandering off to new places. And speaking of the Deathless, they live in a world that will insist on having things happen without them. Hence I disagree with the notion that the game has no room for a supplement for things outside their experience, that they have trouble tracing to their origins.<br /> <br /> Non-Iremite mummies - or other undead or living people infused with Sekhem - aren't the only things a book of that kind could have, either. Relics that don't fit into the Iremite craft guilts, so no Deathless is resistant to their curse. Amkhata partaking of &quot;foreign&quot; animals. There's apparently a huge great mouth under the Sahara - is there anything like that anywhere else? Going back to mummies, they don't have to be beings either - bodies are often mummified because the individual was venerated in life, or else the individual becomes venerated in death because their body mummified - either way, mummies make excellent vestiges or uter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-03T04:56:59-07:00","id":906321,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jacob</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905396#post905396\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you have some problematic assumptions here. One thing that strikes me is that CofD games are all sandbox games and that they have a means of varying their rules and settings called Twists.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The second problem is that mummification was practiced by many cultures. North Africa hardly has a monopoly on the practice. Repeated arguments against is more than a little white-washy. It would be sad for OPP to develop a &quot;white&quot; problem like the MCU has. </div> </div> </div>Nobody's saying Egyptians (or Iremites) own the concept of deliberately preserving bodies of the deceased (though even if someone did, I don't think &quot;whitewashing&quot; would be an appropriate word, seeing how ancient Egyptians weren't exactly white, and neither are Iremites). But this game isn't about preserved corpses - it's about mummies as the staple of horror genre, and this particular brand of mummies is distinctly Egyptian. This is a statement of fact, cultural sensitivity has nothing to it - there's no folklore of mummies rising as undead anywhere in the world, Egypt included, because for the early cultures, elaborate funeral rites served to make sure the dead stayed dead, not the other way around. Mummy as an undead monster is a product of European culture inspired by Egyptomania that overtook Europe and the US in the late 19th - early 20th century. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Third, Iremites must be the best secret keepers ever because the secret never gets out, but if you know anything about how technological innovation propagates across the world then it really doesn't make sense. No one's achievements are unique. Things get invented in multiple places all the time. Sometimes simultaneously. </div> </div> </div>1. Magic isn't necessarily a technology in CofD (though it can be).<br /> <br /> 2. There's a difference between technology and application - many cultures figured out how to build giant statues, but there was only one Colossus of Rhodes. Even if there was ever someone whose understanding of Sekhem matched that of Shan'iatu (which I find highly unlikely, seeing how they're basically demigods who got their knowledge straight from the source, so to speak), it doesn't necessarily follow they must have attempted their own version of the Rite of Return. Likewise, even if other forms of magic existing in CofD could be used to create some sort of Deathless being, it doesn't necessarily follow that such attempts were ever made, or that they were successful.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So yes, I'd expect a 2e storytellers guide to address this obvious gap. Mummy has an unique place in that it's the most obviously linked to a single specific extinct culture. However, that also makes it a game in the most precarious position of looking racist by priveleging one extinct culture over countless others. I'd hate my favorite 1e game to become the elephant in the room. </div> </div> </div>Actually, the opposite is true - the one truly culturally insensitive statement that can be made here is assuming that any RL civilization that mummified their dead intended to animate them with dark magic, which is what people are advocating when they root for non-Iremite mummies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-03T18:16:10-07:00","id":906861,"title":"","content":"&quot;Nobody's saying Egyptians (or Iremites) own the concept of deliberately preserving bodies of the deceased (though even if someone did, I don't think &quot;whitewashing&quot; would be an appropriate word, seeing how ancient Egyptians weren't exactly white, and neither are Iremites). But this game isn't about preserved corpses - it's about mummies as the staple of horror genre, and this particular brand of mummies is distinctly Egyptian. This is a statement of fact, cultural sensitivity has nothing to it - there's no folklore of mummies rising as undead anywhere in the world, Egypt included, because for the early cultures, elaborate funeral rites served to make sure the dead stayed dead, not the other way around. Mummy as an undead monster is a product of European culture inspired by Egyptomania that overtook Europe and the US in the late 19th - early 20th century.&quot;<br /> <br /> That's not entirely accurate. There are two examples of Egyptian dead rising from their graves that I can think of. One was the myth of Osiris being resurrected (and killed again). The other, which I suspect inspired in part the original mummy horror movie, was the tale of Ka-Nefer-Ptah and involved a magical scroll that raised his spirit or body (depending on interpretation). But yes, generally cultural mythology involving something rising from the dead meant something had gone very, very wrong. By the same token, the desire to escape death and live forever in some fashion (whether here and now or in some afterlife) is something that pops up in many cultures around the world. I would suggest that most cultures who can be said to mummify their dead will have some myths or legends that could be adapted the same way those of Egypt have been. <br /> <br /> &quot;1. Magic isn't necessarily a technology in CofD (though it can be).<br /> <br /> 2. There's a difference between technology and application - many cultures figured out how to build giant statues, but there was only one Colossus of Rhodes. Even if there was ever someone whose understanding of Sekhem matched that of Shan'iatu (which I find highly unlikely, seeing how they're basically demigods who got their knowledge straight from the source, so to speak), it doesn't necessarily follow they must have attempted their own version of the Rite of Return. Likewise, even if other forms of magic existing in CofD could be used to create some sort of Deathless being, it doesn't necessarily follow that such attempts were ever made, or that they were successful.&quot;<br /> <br /> If we accept the general idea that Sekhem is a force that flows from and through life and architecture and responds to formations and monuments then its possible that different cultures were indeed aware of the force and interpreted the phenomena through their own cultural lens (the same way, for example, that we can accept a multitude of sun gods or moon gods- every culture has their own explanation for these universal forces). Or come at it from the opposite stand point- where there is a society known for building or geometry (sacred or otherwise) Sekhem will be shaped and respond to their patterns and designs. So some awareness or manipulation is always possible even by chance. Beyond that, we can't be sure in any way that the phenomena of gods essentially walking amongst humanity was isolated to Irem. In the context of the game it is perfectly possible that the Judges are not the only cosmic beings with an interest in guiding humanity or some subgroup thereof down their own inexplicable (or sinister) paths. <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">&quot;Actually, the opposite is true - the one truly culturally insensitive statement that can be made here is assuming that any RL civilization that mummified their dead intended to animate them with dark magic, which is what people are advocating when they root for non-Iremite mummies.&quot;<br /> <br /> But taking a fictional civilization clearly borrowed from a real one and claiming that IT mummified its dead for the purposes of bringing them back to life as servants is just fine? Okay, fine then. Let's assume that a plethora of beyond ancient cultures unknown to modern history but with aspects later civilizations will inherit performed these rituals, knowingly or unknowingly akin to the Rite of Return. Problem solved....? </span><br /> </span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T04:59:04-07:00","id":907062,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Morangias</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=906321#post906321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">... which is what people are advocating when they root for non-Iremite mummies.</div> </div> </div> </div>Incorrect.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T11:57:27-07:00","id":907215,"title":"","content":"I linked this quote earlier in the thread, but I'm actually gonna put it up here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malcolm Sheppard</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Mummy&quot; is not a generic archetypal monster even if the same term is used in English for a variety of preserved bodies. Having Incan mummies is a thematically incoherent addition to the game for the sake of a linguistic quirk. Mummy: The Curse is not about how it's weird that English uses a word derived from a slang term for Egyptian preserved corpses for all preserved corpses.</div> </div> </div> </div>And if you're wondering what his credentials are, go read every Curse book ever.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T15:10:56-07:00","id":907298,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=878681#post878681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Truth be told, I don't necessarily want the civilizations happening side-by-side, so to speak, but I had an idea of a God-Machine plot opening rifts to timelines where Irem never existed, and the Rite was performed by other proto-civilizations. To that end, I'm trying to put together non-Iremite mummies with minimal mechanical divergence.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is the only rationale for non-Iremite Deathless that makes sense to me: a <b>Mirrors</b>-like set of Shards asking \u201cwhat if the roots of the Arisen had been [insert proto-culture here] instead of pre-Egyptian?\u201d","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T19:34:42-07:00","id":907388,"title":"","content":"I could live with that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T20:49:42-07:00","id":907422,"title":"","content":"So if we <i>were</i> to do some Arisen Shards transplanting the Nameless Empire to other places and times in the ancient world, what would be some decent eras to which to shift the focus, and how might that shift affect the game? I'm figuring that in order for them to still be Deathless, the Sekhem/Memory dynamics need to remain in place, save for the \u201cSekhem\u201d name. The mythology behind them would be different, though: different gods, something else in place of the Judges, possibly different Decrees and Pillars (which in turn potentially restructures the Affinities and Utterances), and probably different Guilds. I could see keeping the Decrees and Pillars, just reflavoring their names according to whatever culture you're substituting for Egypt (and possibly reinterpreting the Pillars from \u201cfive parts of the soul\u201d to \u201cfive characteristics of the soul\u201d); especially since that minimizes the work needed with respect to Affinities and Utterances. But the Gods and the Guilds? Those need to change. <br /> <br /> That's still a lot to replace; but I think that as long as you keep the Descent (wake up with 10 \u201cLife Force\u201d, try to accomplish your goals* before it drops to zero, then go catatonic for centuries or millennia until something triggers a new Descent), it will still be similar enough to MtC to count as a MtC Shard. <br /> <br /> As for what cultures might qualify as alternate roots for the Arisen, I think they need to meet three criteria: <br /> <br /> 1. ancient. I'd say no more recent than 1000 B.C.<br /> 2. preserved their dead. Otherwise, you don't have corpses to return to life.<br /> 3. displayed an interest in death and the afterlife. That is, you need a culture where a fictionalized predecessor could have developed the know-how to perform a Rite of Return. <br /> <br /> Other than Egypt, what cultures meet these criteria? <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:gray\">* where goals include building up Memory on the one hand, and doing some sort of \u201cresource gathering\u201d for whatever the root culture's \u201cAfterlife\u201d is on the other hand.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 06-05-2016, 12:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T20:55:53-07:00","id":907427,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=905594#post905594\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Except, it isn't at all racist. Arisen, Shuankhsen, and Deceived all originate from the Rite of Return, which was, by nature, performed once. Irem built that Wonder of The World, no one else got to first. Facts of (Fictional) Life. Trying to water things down by saying one group's achievement was replicated by disparate other cultures, just because they happened to preserve their dead is... I don't know the word for it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Disingenuous? EDIT: Nope, still the wrong word as it turns out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-05T02:25:03-07:00","id":907538,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=907422#post907422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So if we <i>were</i> to do some Arisen Shards transplanting the Nameless Empire to other places and times in the ancient world, what would be some decent eras to which to shift the focus, and how might that shift affect the game? I'm figuring that in order for them to still be Deathless, the Sekhem/Memory dynamics need to remain in place, save for the \u201cSekhem\u201d name. The mythology behind them would be different, though: different gods, something else in place of the Judges, possibly different Decrees and Pillars (which in turn potentially restructures the Affinities and Utterances), and probably different Guilds. I could see keeping the Decrees and Pillars, just reflavoring their names according to whatever culture you're substituting for Egypt (and possibly reinterpreting the Pillars from \u201cfive parts of the soul\u201d to \u201cfive characteristics of the soul\u201d); especially since that minimizes the work needed with respect to Affinities and Utterances. But the Gods and the Guilds? Those need to change. <br /> <br /> That's still a lot to replace; but I think that as long as you keep the Descent (wake up with 10 \u201cLife Force\u201d, try to accomplish your goals* before it drops to zero, then go catatonic for centuries or millennia until something triggers a new Descent), it will still be similar enough to MtC to count as a MtC Shard. <br /> <br /> As for what cultures might qualify as alternate roots for the Arisen, I think they need to meet three criteria: <br /> <br /> 1. ancient. I'd say no more recent than 1000 B.C.<br /> 2. preserved their dead. Otherwise, you don't have corpses to return to life.<br /> 3. displayed an interest in death and the afterlife. That is, you need a culture where a fictionalized predecessor could have developed the know-how to perform a Rite of Return. <br /> <br /> Other than Egypt, what cultures meet these criteria? <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:10px\"><span style=\"color:gray\">* where goals include building up Memory on the one hand, and doing some sort of \u201cresource gathering\u201d for whatever the root culture's \u201cAfterlife\u201d is on the other hand.</span></span></div> </div> </div> </div>I might suggest the first dynasty of China, the semi-mythical Xia? We even have an easy analogue for the fivefold soul in the Five Elements of classic Chinese mysticism, and while they're a later creation there's enough creative space to work within. Descriptions of the Xian or immortals date back several hundred years BCE, while our new Arisen can easily be drawn from the devoted ranks of self-mummifying monks or else be living terracotta warriors.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-05T06:05:14-07:00","id":907582,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=907215#post907215\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I linked this quote earlier in the thread, but I'm actually gonna put it up here.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an answer to Mummy the Curse having non-Iremite mummies in the same way that Mummy the Resurrection had non-Amenti mummies, but it's not a good answer to many of the ideas presented in this thread.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/321-sunlessnick","name":"SunlessNick","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1214"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-05T08:06:06-07:00","id":907638,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=907422#post907422\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for what cultures might qualify as alternate roots for the Arisen, I think they need to meet three criteria:<br /> <br /> 1. ancient. I'd say no more recent than 1000 B.C.<br /> 2. preserved their dead. Otherwise, you don't have corpses to return to life.<br /> 3. displayed an interest in death and the afterlife. That is, you need a culture where a fictionalized predecessor could have developed the know-how to perform a Rite of Return.<br /> <br /> Other than Egypt, what cultures meet these criteria?</div> </div> </div> </div> I agree with points 1 and 3, but I think there should be some wiggle room with point 2. If we're going to continue basing the Arisen off of fictional ancestor cultures, cultural drift would doubtless take place, and how a particular &quot;seed culture&quot; treats its dead might not have much, if any, bearing on the practices of the proto-culture.<br /> <br /> For example, the Ancestral Pueblo Peoples, <i>aka</i> the Anazazi. They had complex indoor burials with gifts interred with the dead, and there is substantial belief in the religious significance of the Great North Road &quot;leading to their place of origin and along which the spirits of the dead travel.&quot; (Wikipedia). This sounds like as good a seed-culture for Arisen as any.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-07T13:10:13-07:00","id":909408,"title":"","content":"You really have very little material on South America.<br /> <br /> There is a whole literature and horror film involving things like mummies in Paraguay and Argentina derived from Andean sources like Mummies.<br /> <br /> We also have pre-civilizational records of more than four millennia of burials with ritual mummification in the Andes.<br /> <br /> Said that four things have to be taken into account:<br /> <br /> - Nothing prevents the information that Iremitas have are lies of their Gods / Judges and other Gods as terrible as they have done different but so powerful rituals as in 2 or 3 parts of the world, with its advantages and disadvantages in relation to ritual Namaless.<br /> <br /> - Must be separated which egipicias characteristics of the features added by Onyx Path horror of Iremitas in order to keep the latter and remove the first.<br /> <br /> - Other Mummies do not need to be thought in templates 5 x 5.<br /> <br /> - Discounting differences clans, in which all Kindred however different it is from each other, have the same structure, the Dead Wicked playable are 25% to 30% different from Kindred and each other. This can be maintained.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/641-ben-linus","name":"Ben Linus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"444"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-08T03:36:10-07:00","id":909956,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ben Linus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=909408#post909408\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You really have very little material on South America.<br /> <br /> There is a whole literature and horror film involving things like mummies in Paraguay and Argentina derived from Andean sources like Mummies.</div> </div> </div> </div>These are, as far as I've managed to study the topic, localized remakes of the European and American stuff that shoehorn the pre-Columbian mummies in the same roles that originally starred the Egyptian dead. They are also extremely niche, with no noticeable cultural impact outside their points of origin.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We also have pre-civilizational records of more than four millennia of burials with ritual mummification in the Andes. </div> </div> </div>Which is, again, irrelevant, because of the different meanings of the term &quot;mummy&quot; that have been discussed to death.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> - Nothing prevents the information that Iremitas have are lies of their Gods / Judges and other Gods as terrible as they have done different but so powerful rituals as in 2 or 3 parts of the world, with its advantages and disadvantages in relation to ritual Namaless. </div> </div> </div>And doesn't that strike you as terribly contrived that not one, but several ancient civilizations have not only sought to make immortal servants for their horrible gods, but all chose a preserved corpses to be the physical vessels of said servants?<br /> <br /> More generally speaking, doesn't that strike you as terribly contrived and symmetrical for the sole sake of symmetry to force a horrible magical predecessor empire worshiping horrible gods and dabbling with forces beyond human ken on every ancient civilization that happened to produce preserved corpses more or less consciously?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/582-morangias","name":"Morangias","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar582_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1670"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-08T12:46:38-07:00","id":910255,"title":"","content":"Here's something that occurred to me: the Shan'iatu created the Rite of Return from first principles. Nothing like it had vbeen tried before, <b>ever</b>. As such, who is to say that the Rite may not have had unintended side effects, effects that other civiliations (or perhaps Fate Itself) could take advantage of, however unwittingly? That way it isn't because a given civilization had the backing of UltraTerrestrial Beings that immortal living/dead beings have been made; Fate ordains that such beings are possible, just as such had been ordained to Fate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/665-shkspr1048","name":"shkspr1048","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"630"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-08T20:31:39-07:00","id":910519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>shkspr1048</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=910255#post910255\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here's something that occurred to me: the Shan'iatu created the Rite of Return from first principles.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, no, they were given the Rite by an intermediary and executed it according to those instructions.<br /> <br /> You may recall who that intermediary spoke for.<br /> <br /> <i>Go forth! You are not the only one to have dreamed thus.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":". 06-09-2016, 03:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-11T14:43:01-07:00","id":912449,"title":"","content":"&quot;These are, as far as I've managed to study the topic, localized remakes of the European and American stuff that shoehorn the pre-Columbian mummies in the same roles that originally starred the Egyptian dead. They are also extremely niche, with no noticeable cultural impact outside their points of origin.&quot;<br /> <br /> I don't think we should be counting that against such movies. If we do, we have to reject any number of other horror films for 'borrowing' some or all of their tropes from earlier classics, after all. Nosferatu, for instance, is pretty much the story of Dracula with the names changed and an uglier face for the monster. In fact, I'd argue that the entire reason we have iconic monsters is because many directors become inspired (or get lazy) and adapt their films to match the most famous examples.<br /> <br /> &quot;Which is, again, irrelevant, because of the different meanings of the term &quot;mummy&quot; that have been discussed to death.&quot;<br /> <br /> Discussed to death, indeed. But to my mind one of the scariest aspects of The Mummy as a monster is the age. It is a being from another lost time, one which we know of only it glimpses and guesses. Everything about it, from its gods to its history and culture and its desires and agendas are lost to time. It alone remembers what came before, and unfortunately for modern men the key to sending it back to death often in the scraps of knowledge gleaned from that same ancient civilization. This is Lovecraft before Lovecraft was cool. <br /> What I'm saying, in my long winded way, is that The Mummy SHOULD be ancient, on the scale of thousands of years. And can you tell what kind of corpse tends to survive for thousands of years without turning to dust? The answer is (drum roll please)....ones that are mummified. My point is, whatever you want to name your monster- if you are telling a story about something truly ancient rising from its grave to terrorize the living (as opposed to something dormant or ageless, mind you) the kind of walking corpse you are most likely to describe is a preserved one. E.G.- a mummy. Its hardly a big leap in logic to make your mummy into The Mummy. If you get my meaning.<br /> <br /> &quot;And doesn't that strike you as terribly contrived that not one, but several ancient civilizations have not only sought to make immortal servants for their horrible gods, but all chose a preserved corpses to be the physical vessels of said servants?&quot;<br /> <br /> That specifically? Sure. But the reasons behind the creation of the ritual don't have to be that specific. Death is terrifying in its finality. Unsurprisingly, it has thus occupied the minds and myths of civilizations for as long as we have had them. The ways in which early man dealt with his dead are many and varied, but they all held that singular fascination and dread of what came next. We already know of at least one great ancient empire that rivaled Irem- Ubar the proto-Mesopotamian civilization. There is no reason to assume there were not others. Not all of them have to have crafted a Rite of Return for the reasons Irem did. In fact, I'd say any competing Rite should have a purpose unique to the civilization it came from. But the idea that Death would occupy their lives as much as it did Irem's people, enough to turn their magics and studies to the dead, the afterlife, and methods for circumnavigating or circumventing both? Why is that implausible?<br /> &quot;More generally speaking, doesn't that strike you as terribly contrived and symmetrical for the sole sake of symmetry to force a horrible magical predecessor empire worshiping horrible gods and dabbling with forces beyond human ken on every ancient civilization that happened to produce preserved corpses more or less consciously?&quot;<br /> <br /> Humans in every civilization have prayed to gods for protection and guidance. Every time, every place. Does it strike me as surprising that in the World of Darkness, especially the ancient world where the barriers between life, death, and spirit were not as constraining as they are in the modern age, that the things most likely to listen and respond to those ancient prayers are scary and terrible and unknowable? Is that a trick question?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-11T17:31:00-07:00","id":912546,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=912449#post912449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Snip</div> </div> </div> </div>It feels like we're getting a bit carried away here. It's perfectly easy to justify monsters based on the mummified remains of other cultures. That's never been an issue. Even the Mummy core book references the existence of non-Arisen Mummies. Read the Blasphemous Depiction's write-up. It basically says, &quot;Hey, if you want Bog Mummies in your chronicle, this thing is a good justification&quot;.<br /> <br /> Where most people seem to actually disagree though is on the subject of using the Arisen template as the basis for non-Arisen Mummies. It's a template that is pretty unique by the standards of WoD. It includes a range of culturally specific and internally justified mechanics, which leads to issues of adapting it to other types of Mummies. You have to justify either using culturally specific mechanics outside the origin culture or continuing to use the Arisen template as the basis of your new being despite the fact that you have to change a raft of mechanics. For those elements (particularly the Descent and Memory mechanics) that are internally justified by the story of Irem, you need to justify why such specific similarities were the result of convergent evolution.<br /> <br /> Thus far, the best argument I've heard is &quot;Mummies should be ancient horrors, and the Arisen template has mechanics like Sybaris that emphasise this horror&quot;. Which is fine, but I'm not sure it's enough on its own. Whether it be because the relevant age of Iremite Mummies is considerably greater than Incan ones, or that Sybaris on its own seems easier to add to Revenant mechanics than changing Pillars, Utterances, Affinities, and justifying the Descent on the Arisen template, or simply that Arisen have already done the Sybaris thing, and it may be more interesting to create a wholly new mechanic for emphasising the weight of ages on others, I can think of many arguments that weaken the strength of the initial point. Particularly the latter. The impact Arisen have on others is a relatively minor part of their template. The impact of their age is mostly felt internally, through the Descent and Memory.<br /> <br /> I don't think the contentiousness of non-Iremite Mummies has ever been over whether or not they should <i>exist</i>. Rather, the objection is that people latch on to Mummy: The Curse as the best place to create them, when (as argued by others in this thread) Prometheans, Vampires, Geists, and the ghost mechanics all present equally good, even better places to start from. I'm sure a &quot;non-Egyptian Mummy&quot; topic would be embraced with open arms, including by many in this thread, if it were posted in the general forums rather than here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T06:02:11-07:00","id":912753,"title":"","content":"What makes non-Arisen Mummies interesting to see in <b>Mummy the Curse</b> to see how the themes challenge the Deathless in their elitist perspective, you know? Or for people that don't include crossover in their games; I don't include very much, for example, so I don't want to have to dig through <b>Geist</b> which I have zero interest in","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T14:46:54-07:00","id":913008,"title":"","content":"&quot;It feels like we're getting a bit carried away here. It's perfectly easy to justify monsters based on the mummified remains of other cultures. That's never been an issue. Even the Mummy core book references the existence of non-Arisen Mummies. Read the Blasphemous Depiction's write-up. It basically says, &quot;Hey, if you want Bog Mummies in your chronicle, this thing is a good justification&quot;. &quot;<br /> <br /> Well, that's one way to interpret the text. Another is 'you know all those other bog mummies? Yeah, none of them are actually alive. Its only the ones enchanted by this thing that are real.' But I get your point.<br /> <br /> &quot;Where most people seem to actually disagree though is on the subject of using the Arisen template as the basis for non-Arisen Mummies. It's a template that is pretty unique by the standards of WoD. It includes a range of culturally specific and internally justified mechanics, which leads to issues of adapting it to other types of Mummies. You have to justify either using culturally specific mechanics outside the origin culture or continuing to use the Arisen template as the basis of your new being despite the fact that you have to change a raft of mechanics. For those elements (particularly the Descent and Memory mechanics) that are internally justified by the story of Irem, you need to justify why such specific similarities were the result of convergent evolution.&quot;<br /> <br /> I admit the task is more daunting than in other games (I'll leave aside the discussion about whether this is a flaw of the game or not- though I will once again voice my opinion that the tool kit approach has worked marvelously in other games and I miss the lack of it here). However, I don't think the problem is insurmountable. If we were willing to take the template apart and look at it piece by piece I think we could identify the working parts that don't need changing and those that do. The concept of the Pillars, for instance, is something I think could remain broadly the same (piece of a mummy's 'soul' ranked from one to five that powers his supernatural abilities) while specifically distinct (for instance, perhaps a mummy derived from a Chinese culture has a 'Po' pillar and a 'Hun' pillar since some of their philosophies divided a person's being into those two pieces). Yes, this idea is basically a take off of Mummy: The Resurrection and the non-Egyptian 'Decrees' seen in that setting. But you get the idea. I think we can keep many of the broad aspects and change many of the specific details. As for Descent and Memory I'm utterly failing to see the problem. Memory has been around as far back as WOD: Mummy. Each edition has a slightly different version of the concept, yeah, but overall it functions the same. I mean, look- broadly speaking we are presumably talking about an entity at least several thousand years old that oscillates between life and death and has seen endless successions of friends and lovers live and die. Remembering what it was like to live, why morality mattered to it, and the indescribable horrors of the underworld alone justify a mummy's shaky grasp on what has come before in my mind. Sure, the Judges place a greater emphasis on shutting down a Mummy's memories than others might- perhaps non-iremites should start with more than 3 dots of Memory to reflect this? Just spit balling here. All I'm saying is that you can have your story elements and still keep Memory for more alien Mummies. The Descent is even easier in my opinion. Returning what was dead back to life is taxing. Allowing that thing to appear human? More so. Allowing it to return again and again when killed? More so still. It makes sense that what allows a Mummy to walk in the living world would slowly ebb away- the underworld might simply not be equipped to keep such an entity going indefinitely like that. I mean, the Shuankhsen have a limited span too, and unlike the Judges I don't see Ammut having any real qualms about letting her servants reek havoc amongst the living indefinitely if she had a choice in the matter. Again, the specifics should change a little, especially what offenses will cause a Mummy's descent roll to activate sooner and what actions will help prolong their existence. But again, the broad concepts don't really need rearranging in my mind. If you disagree please share with me your reasoning.<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;Thus far, the best argument I've heard is &quot;Mummies should be ancient horrors, and the Arisen template has mechanics like Sybaris that emphasise this horror&quot;. Which is fine, but I'm not sure it's enough on its own. Whether it be because the relevant age of Iremite Mummies is considerably greater than Incan ones, or that Sybaris on its own seems easier to add to Revenant mechanics than changing Pillars, Utterances, Affinities, and justifying the Descent on the Arisen template, or simply that Arisen have already done the Sybaris thing, and it may be more interesting to create a wholly new mechanic for emphasising the weight of ages on others, I can think of many arguments that weaken the strength of the initial point. Particularly the latter. The impact Arisen have on others is a relatively minor part of their template. The impact of their age is mostly felt internally, through the Descent and Memory.&quot;<br /> <br /> Why is it necessary to &quot;argue&quot; the point at all? This is what some of the fans of this community are interested in doing. There was a time I could jump into one of these forums and offer up my idea and get suggestions and support back from the community. Now it feels like doing so is asking to be slapped in the face. Maybe Mummy is a victim of its own success. Maybe it took the isolated concept of Egyptian style immortals and did so well with it that we're now afraid to go in new directions lest we somehow shatter the apparently fragile foundations of the core game. I dunno. I just feel like a lot of energy has been lost solely to shoot down suggestions like this one. And that sucks.<br /> <br /> &quot;I don't think the contentiousness of non-Iremite Mummies has ever been over whether or not they should <i>exist</i>. Rather, the objection is that people latch on to Mummy: The Curse as the best place to create them, when (as argued by others in this thread) Prometheans, Vampires, Geists, and the ghost mechanics all present equally good, even better places to start from. I'm sure a &quot;non-Egyptian Mummy&quot; topic would be embraced with open arms, including by many in this thread, if it were posted in the general forums rather than here.&quot;<br /> <br /> Doesn't it strike you as just a LITTLE fucked up that a thread about how to create a mummy from culture X is expected to be better received in practically any other forum than the one that has MUMMY written in the title of the game? We should be able to freely discuss new ideas and concepts for THIS game and THESE mechanics without receiving a barrage of hostility in return.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T16:09:15-07:00","id":913059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=913008#post913008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Doesn't it strike you as just a LITTLE fucked up that a thread about how to create a mummy from culture X is expected to be better received in practically any other forum than the one that has MUMMY written in the title of the game? We should be able to freely discuss new ideas and concepts for THIS game and THESE mechanics without receiving a barrage of hostility in return.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm addressing this one first, because it's kind of the driving impetus behind this entire topic. If you look back in this thread, you will see a quote from one of Mummy's own writers about, essentially, the fact that the only link between an Iremite Mummy and any other Mummy is that English uses the same word to describe these highly distinct phenomena. This means that, no, I don't think it's fucked up that creating a Mummy from Culture X is better received in a forum that lacks a specific link to Egyptian culture. Similarly, I don't think there's anything wrong with saying &quot;These Mummy mechanics were intended solely for use with the specifically Egyptian iteration of preserved corpses&quot;. Just because two things are <i>called</i> &quot;Mummies&quot; doesn't mean that they're even remotely the same thing.<br /> <br /> And that is why these topics generate calls to take them elsewhere. A lot of the traditions and mythology around Incan Mummies actually make them better represented by the likes of Ulgan Prometheans than by Arisen with some name swapping.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=913008#post913008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I admit the task is more daunting than in other games (I'll leave aside the discussion about whether this is a flaw of the game or not- though I will once again voice my opinion that the tool kit approach has worked marvelously in other games and I miss the lack of it here). However, I don't think the problem is insurmountable. If we were willing to take the template apart and look at it piece by piece I think we could identify the working parts that don't need changing and those that do. The concept of the Pillars, for instance, is something I think could remain broadly the same (piece of a mummy's 'soul' ranked from one to five that powers his supernatural abilities) while specifically distinct (for instance, perhaps a mummy derived from a Chinese culture has a 'Po' pillar and a 'Hun' pillar since some of their philosophies divided a person's being into those two pieces). Yes, this idea is basically a take off of Mummy: The Resurrection and the non-Egyptian 'Decrees' seen in that setting. But you get the idea. I think we can keep many of the broad aspects and change many of the specific details.</div> </div> </div> </div>At a certain point, you have to ask yourself why you are doing such a thing. Is it because you genuinely believe that this better suits your idea of what a non-Egyptian Mummy is than, say, a variant on the Essence/Vitae/Glamour/Plasm/whatever rules that all other beings in the World of Darkness use? Or is it because you've seen the word &quot;Mummy&quot; in the game's title and, because the word &quot;Mummy&quot; is also used to describe the flavour of supernatural being you want to create, you've decided that it has to mirror as many elements of existing mummies as possible?<br /> <br /> When the rituals used to create a thing are so different, the reasons behind creating them so different, the circumstances of their creation so removed, the question that needs to be answered is, &quot;Why are they so similar?&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=913008#post913008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for Descent and Memory I'm utterly failing to see the problem. Memory has been around as far back as WOD: Mummy. Each edition has a slightly different version of the concept, yeah, but overall it functions the same. I mean, look- broadly speaking we are presumably talking about an entity at least several thousand years old that oscillates between life and death and has seen endless successions of friends and lovers live and die. Remembering what it was like to live, why morality mattered to it, and the indescribable horrors of the underworld alone justify a mummy's shaky grasp on what has come before in my mind. Sure, the Judges place a greater emphasis on shutting down a Mummy's memories than others might- perhaps non-iremites should start with more than 3 dots of Memory to reflect this? Just spit balling here. All I'm saying is that you can have your story elements and still keep Memory for more alien Mummies. The Descent is even easier in my opinion. Returning what was dead back to life is taxing. Allowing that thing to appear human? More so. Allowing it to return again and again when killed? More so still. It makes sense that what allows a Mummy to walk in the living world would slowly ebb away- the underworld might simply not be equipped to keep such an entity going indefinitely like that. I mean, the Shuankhsen have a limited span too, and unlike the Judges I don't see Ammut having any real qualms about letting her servants reek havoc amongst the living indefinitely if she had a choice in the matter. Again, the specifics should change a little, especially what offenses will cause a Mummy's descent roll to activate sooner and what actions will help prolong their existence. But again, the broad concepts don't really need rearranging in my mind. If you disagree please share with me your reasoning.</div> </div> </div> </div>My primary &quot;disagreement&quot; is that you've just made a refluffed Arisen. That's... honestly kind of boring. Arisen are unique in the World of Darkness, almost a direct inversion of a standard World of Darkness template. By mimicking them, it feels like you feel constrained by the term &quot;Mummy&quot;. Your comment about the name of this forum really gives the impression that you aren't able to get past the fact that, because these two things share a name, they should share mechanics. Why? We already have a game for players experiencing immortality from the perspective of a slowly-weakening amnesiacs. I've been suggesting Revenants as a good starting point for something new, because they're exceptionally generic and consequently easy to mod. You don't need to remove much, only add.<br /> <br /> If in-universe lore matters (I'm never sure if it does in these conversations, fanon doesn't need to adhere to canon for obvious reasons) then there are admittedly quite a few obvious points to argue. The Rite created exceptionally powerful creatures, ones that have been beyond the reach of successor cultures, hence the whole &quot;start at 10 and descend&quot; mechanic for Sekhem. Having creatures with equivalent power dilutes that. Similarly, you say that &quot;Returning what was dead back to life is taxing&quot;. Sure. Please note the dozen different species of undead in the World of Darkness that use completely different mechanics from Arisen. Some of these types even have variations on hibernation/returning from being killed. They don't all use the Descent. Logically, this means there are a variety of techniques for creating undead. Not all need to follow the same rituals and limitations chosen by ancient necromancers in pre-dynastic Egypt. In fact, the more similarities there are to this Rite that has never been replicated, the more suspension of disbelief is stretched.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nothri</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/876960-on-non-iremite-mummies?p=913008#post913008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why is it necessary to &quot;argue&quot; the point at all? This is what some of the fans of this community are interested in doing. There was a time I could jump into one of these forums and offer up my idea and get suggestions and support back from the community. Now it feels like doing so is asking to be slapped in the face. Maybe Mummy is a victim of its own success. Maybe it took the isolated concept of Egyptian style immortals and did so well with it that we're now afraid to go in new directions lest we somehow shatter the apparently fragile foundations of the core game. I dunno. I just feel like a lot of energy has been lost solely to shoot down suggestions like this one. And that sucks.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mummy was an intentionally limited game. It's extremely focussed. It works very well within that focus. You can make veiled accusations of cowardice among its fans all you want, but that's the reality we're working with.<br /> <br /> Personally, part of the frustration I have with making non-Egyptian Mummies based on Mummy: The Curse is because it feels like the writers of such attempts are getting caught up on the name. It feels like a lot of potential design space is squandered the moment that happens, meaning we get a cavalcade of pseudo-Arisen instead of anything new.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-15T09:53:34-07:00","id":914885,"title":"","content":"I like that &quot;shards&quot; idea, tbh. It's something I wish ChroD had more of in general.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5358-geckopirateship","name":"Geckopirateship","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5358_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1383"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}